const STORY_DATA = [{"mission":0,"part":null,"text":"A tall and rather slim man in a lab coat adjusts his glasses as you crest a hill bringing him into view. He glances briefly toward the small pink Pokémon quietly observing beside him.\n\n“Welcome, trainers. Thank you for agreeing to take part in this rather unusual research expedition. I am Professor Cypress, a lifelong student of Pokémon history. Across centuries of records—ruins, myths, field journals, and oral traditions—we have pieced together a remarkably detailed account of the world’s past. We know when certain Pokémon first appeared in legend, when ancient conflicts occurred, and even when entire regions rose or fell.”\n\nHe pauses, folding his hands thoughtfully.\n\n“And yet… knowledge written in books is not the same as knowledge lived.”\n\n“The events that shaped Pokémon history were experienced by real trainers, real Pokémon, in real moments of uncertainty and discovery. Those moments cannot be fully understood from dusty archives alone.”\n\nCypress gestures calmly toward the Pokemon beside him.\n\n“With the help of my companion Mew here, we have developed a way to observe these pivotal points in history safely, and without disturbing the natural course of events.”\n\n“Your role is simple: step into these moments, face the challenges other trainers once faced, and help us understand history not just as a record… but as an experience.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":0,"part":"a","text":"Professor Cypress clasps his hands behind his back, studying the group with a measured, almost excited curiosity.\n\n“Before any great journey through history can begin, a trainer must first have a partner. No explorer—past or present—has ever succeeded alone.”\n\nHe gestures toward the quiet figure floating beside him.\n\n“With Mew’s unique abilities, we can do more than simply tell you about the past. We can recreate it—the environments, the trainers, the battles, and the Pokémon themselves. What you experience will feel real in every sense that matters.”\n\nCypress begins pacing slowly as he continues.\n\n“Throughout your journey, you will encounter many Pokémon—indeed, every known species recorded in our world’s history. When you meet them, you may choose to add them to your team. However, there is one important constraint: once a Pokémon has joined a trainer, that specific partner is no longer available to others. In this way, each team will grow in its own unique direction.”\n\nHe stops and smiles faintly.\n\n“So, trainers… it is time for the most important decision any of you will make.”\n\n“Take your first steps forward and meet the Pokémon who will begin this journey with you.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":1,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress consults a small tablet, nodding as if confirming the results of an experiment.\n\n“Excellent. The bond between trainer and partner forms quickly when the moment is right. With two Pokémon at your side, you now carry the same responsibility every trainer throughout history has faced—the responsibility to learn alongside them.”\n\nHe gestures outward, and the environment around you seems to subtly shift and settle.\n\n“For your first field exercise, we will begin somewhere very familiar in the records of Pokémon training: Route One. Across countless regions and generations, trainers have taken their earliest steps along roads just like this one. Tall grass, open paths, and Pokémon living freely at the edges of civilization.”\n\nCypress folds his arms thoughtfully.\n\n“These routes were never meant to be dangerous. They were meant to teach. Wild Pokémon here test a trainer’s instincts—how to command a partner, how to react, and how to adapt.”\n\nHe glances briefly toward the quiet presence of Mew beside him.\n\n“So take a breath, step into the grass, and meet the first challenge every trainer must face.”\n\n“Your first wild battle awaits.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":2,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress watches the group carefully as the last echoes of battle fade from the tall grass.\n\n“Well done, trainers. Your first encounter with wild Pokémon has concluded just as the historical records suggest it often does—with quick thinking, a little courage, and the formation of a brand-new partnership.”\n\nHe makes a small note on his tablet before continuing.\n\n“One of the fascinating constants in Pokémon history is growth. Trainers who challenge themselves—and whose Pokémon fight beside them—rarely remain the same for long. Skill sharpens. Bonds strengthen. And Pokémon themselves become more capable with each experience.”\n\nCypress gestures calmly toward the surrounding fields.\n\n“To reflect that principle, each successful mission you complete will strengthen your team. The Pokémon traveling with you will grow slightly more powerful after every assignment. Consider it the natural result of training, battle experience, and the bond you are forming with them.”\n\nHis expression turns thoughtful.\n\n“However, before we begin examining the truly pivotal moments of Pokémon history, we must be certain you are ready.”\n\nHe nods once.\n\n“A few more trials await—proof that both trainer and Pokémon can continue to grow together.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":3,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress reviews the recent encounter data for a moment before looking back to the group.\n\n“The Pokémon you encountered along that route are an important part of the ecosystem, and every species has its own place in the wider history of our world. However,” he adds thoughtfully, “the Pokémon you just encountered are rarely the ones trainers carry with them through their greatest trials, but you will see their value early on.”\n\nHe gestures forward, as the landscape begins to change.\n\n“The next stage of a trainer’s early journey is also one of the most consistent across regions: the forest. Dense, living ecosystems like these have long served as the first true test of a trainer’s adaptability.”\n\nCypress glances briefly toward Mew beside him.\n\n“Step carefully, trainers. The forest is where many journeys truly begin.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":4,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress offers a small, knowing smile.\n\n“Don’t be too quick to assume you understand a place after only a few steps. Forests are often remembered for their many Bug Pokémon, but they are far more diverse than that. Continue exploring carefully—many different Pokémon make their homes beneath the canopy.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":5,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress welcomes you back and exclaims:\n\n“Most trainers begin their journey with a partner at Level 5. It is the point at which a young Pokémon is capable of learning, battling, and forming the bonds that define a trainer’s path.”\n\nHe pauses briefly.\n\n“Throughout history, a few Pokémon have been given a special title by researchers and storytellers alike: ‘the first Pokémon.’ My companion here—Mew—is one such candidate, believed by many to contain the genetic roots of all Pokémon life.”\n\nCypress taps his tablet, and three Poké Balls appear before the trainers.\n\n“Now you will meet another.”\n\n“These partners have guided generations of trainers through their very first battles.”\n\nHe looks up with quiet anticipation.\n\n“Take a moment, consider carefully… and choose your starter.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":6,"part":null,"text":"When you return, Professor Cypress chuckles and gestures at the pokeballs, one of the three pokeballs is now missing. \n\n“30 years ago, two boys began their journey in Professor Oak’s Lab. The first boy, thinking he had the advantage, got to choose his preferred starter. The second boy, though initially upset, realized he could pick the starter who would best counteract his rivals choice.”\n\n“The question for you is… who do you think awaits you?”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":7,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress is sweating, but nods approvingly. Just one pokeball remains.\n\n“Fire starters sure do bring remarkable energy to a team. Though, I really hope this next group will be a little refreshing!”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":8,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress folds his hands thoughtfully as he addresses the trainers.\n\n“While it is true that nearly every region offers the familiar choice between Grass-, Fire-, and Water-type starters, history provides a few notable exceptions. There is one particularly famous example of a trainer who arrived too late to receive a traditional starter and instead formed a partnership with a very different Pokémon—one that would go on to become among the most recognizable in the world.”\n\nHe pauses, allowing the implication to settle.\n\n“Interestingly, researchers have noticed a curious pattern. Across many regions, similar small species have been discovered.”\n\nCypress smiles slightly.\n\n“Let’s see which of these special partners might choose you.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":9,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress’s voice lowers slightly as the scene around you resolves into a quiet, somber town. A tall structure looms ahead.\n\n“You are now approaching one of the more delicate moments recorded in Pokémon history.”\n\nHe gestures toward the looming silhouette of Lavender Tower.\n\n“This tower has long served as a resting place for Pokémon that have passed on. Trainers come here to pay respects, remember their partners, and reflect on the bond they once shared.”\n\nHis expression tightens.\n\n“Unfortunately, not everyone who passes through history does so with honorable intentions.”\n\nNear the tower entrance stand three very familiar figures—the notorious agents of Team Rocket.\n\nAt the front, the dramatic duo Jessie and James spread their arms to block the doorway while their partner, Meowth, eyes you suspiciously.\n\nCypress speaks calmly.\n\n“It appears an incident has already taken place inside the tower. And judging by their behavior… they have no intention of allowing anyone else to investigate.”\n\nHe nods toward the entrance.\n\n“Trainers, it seems you’ll have to persuade them to move.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":9,"part":"a","text":null,"link":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZBVrPWwSlRM","status":null},{"mission":9,"part":"b","text":"Professor Cypress’s voice returns as the tower doors creak open behind you.\n\n“Well handled, trainers. However, it seems the situation here runs deeper than a simple guard at the door.”\n\nInside Lavender Tower stands a more ominous looking Team Rocket grunt—surrounded by drifting, ghostly Pokémon.\n\n“Stay alert. Something here is very wrong.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":10,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress exhales quietly as the tower grows still again.\n\n“What happened here is undeniably tragic. The loss of that Cubone’s mother is one of the more painful moments preserved in the historical record. Pokémon and trainers alike have always shared the same world… and the same hardships.”\n\nHe pauses before continuing.\n\n“Moments like these remind us why understanding history matters. Not simply to remember what happened—but to learn from it, and ensure that the bonds between Pokémon and trainers remain strong.”\n\nCypress straightens, tapping a control on his tablet.\n\n“Our next destination lies far earlier in time. The Johto region once suffered a devastating drought that threatened both people and Pokémon alike. Crops failed, rivers shrank, and the land itself began to struggle.”\n\nHe looks up.\n\n“But one Pokémon rose during that crisis—one still revered today for bringing life back to the region.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":11,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress watches the clouds come back into view as the well’s walls fade away, a thoughtful expression crossing his face.\n\n“Some historians still debate what truly ended the great drought of Johto. Was it coincidence? A shift in weather patterns? Or truly the yawn of a simple Slowpoke?”\n\nHe allows a small smile.\n\n“We choose to believe the tale.”\n\nCypress nods.\n\n“Because it reminds us of something important: greatness does not always come from the strongest or the rarest. Sometimes, even the most unlikely among us can become a hero.”\n\nHe taps his tablet, and the environment begins to shift again.\n\n“Now, trainers, we move to a destination that has excited explorers for generations.”\n\n“A place filled with rare Pokémon and unexpected encounters…”\n\n“The Safari Zone awaits.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":12,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress is still scrolling through his tablet as the tall grass of the Safari Zone fades away. Child-like excitement brightens his eyes.\n\n“I hope you all enjoyed that. Many trainers go their entire lives without ever seeing species like the ones you just met—let alone catching any.”\n\nHe lowers the tablet, and his tone turns more thoughtful.\n\n“When people think of rivalries, they usually think of the trainers. Two young people, one road, and a lifetime spent trying to outdo the other.”\n\n“But there are several examples in the Pokémon world of intense rivalries between specific species. Some feuds are so old that researchers believe the memory of the fight is carried in every generation… passed down long after anyone could remember how it began.”\n\nThe scene settles into a rocky clearing. Somewhere ahead, claws scrape against stone… and something hisses back.\n\nCypress adjusts his glasses, a note of caution in his voice.\n\n“Be careful, trainers. This battle may not go the way you expect—and it won't impact everyone the same way.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":13,"part":null,"text":"“Well. I did warn you it might not go the way you expected.”\n\n“Two species locked in a feud older than memory—and yet the most fascinating moment may have been the quiet one. A single young Rockruff, watching the very same battle, with three very different paths ahead of it.”\n\nHis eyes scan the screen thoughtfully.\n\n“It’s one of my favorite truths in all of Pokémon research: what a creature experiences—how it interprets the world around it—can shape who they become.”\n\nThe rocky clearing melts away. Warm sea air drifts in, and flowers bloom around you—red, yellow, pink, and purple.\n\nCypress’s eyes light up.\n\n“Which brings us somewhere far more cheerful. On these islands lives a small bird that changes completely depending on which flower’s nectar it sips. One species, four very different personalities… and every one of them expresses itself the same way.”\n\nHe attempts a small, slightly awkward shuffle of his feet, then clears his throat.\n\n“Through dance.”\n\n“Step lively, trainers. The islands of Alola await.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":14,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress is still humming a little tune as the island breeze fades, his foot tapping along without him seeming to notice.\n\n“Not every page of history is written by legends, you know. Most of it was written on ordinary routes, by ordinary trainers who simply refused to back down from a challenge.”\n\nThe scene settles onto a quiet dirt path. A lone trainer stands ahead—beside a rather oddly shaped tree.\n\nCypress smiles.\n\n“There’s an old rule among trainers, older than any league. When two trainers’ eyes meet…”\n\nThe trainer turns. Their eyes meet yours.\n\n“…a battle must begin.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":15,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress chuckles as the dirt path fades away.\n\n“Normally you’d collect a few Pokédollars for defeating another trainer. For the sake of this exercise, though, taking their Pokémon like a Rocket grunt will have to suffice.”\n\nThe scene shifts. White walls rise around you, lined with humming machines and blinking monitors. A clinical-looking sign by the door reads Cinnabar Lab.\n\n“About thirty years ago, the Silph Corporation hired a brilliant scientist named Dr. Fuji. His assignment was as bold as it was controversial.”\n\nMew drifts a little closer to Cypress’s shoulder.\n\n“They wanted him to clone Mew.”\n\nHis voice softens.\n\n“My friend here remembers those years well. It isn’t easy to talk about—but Mew wanted you to know.”\n\n“For all their effort, the closest they came was a small, pink, shapeless creature that could become anything it saw… but only ever as an imitation. Today, we call it Ditto.”\n\nHe pauses.\n\n“Then, one night, an explosion tore through one of their labs. The records from that night are incomplete. All anyone knows for certain is that it frightened everyone involved.”\n\n“So the scientists abandoned living tissue altogether. Instead, they built something entirely synthetic—the first modern artificial Pokémon, made of nothing but programming code.”\n\n\"This Pokémon may not be an obvious sign of our hubris, but its creators know it is a direct result of it.\"","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":16,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress lets out a long breath as the lab fades away, then takes off his glasses and rubs his eyes.\n\n“That’s quite enough heavy history for one day, I think.”\n\nSunlight spills across a quiet meadow. A stream babbles somewhere nearby, and a small family of mice scurries through the grass in perfect step.\n\nCypress smiles, already looking lighter.\n\n“Sometimes a trainer just needs a walk in the fresh air. Explore a little. See who you meet.”\n\nHe pauses, then raises a finger.\n\n“Although—if you happen to spot a Poké Ball lying out in the open… I wouldn’t pick it up.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":17,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress plucks a few stray seeds from his coat sleeve, looking rather pleased to have just been in the meadow.\n\n“Shall we keep walking? I promise this next stretch is just as pleasant.”\n\nAs you step forward, the world shifts around you. Cherry blossoms drift past… then summer heat shimmers over the sand… autumn leaves crunch underfoot… and finally, snow begins to fall.\n\nCypress laughs, brushing a snowflake from his glasses.\n\n“Mew does love to show off. Every season in a single afternoon—and some Pokémon here change right along with them.”\n\n“Bundle up, trainers. Or don’t. Just wait a few minutes.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":18,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress shakes the last of the snow from his coat, still smiling.\n\n“Some Pokémon change with the seasons. Others… simply make do with whatever is around them.”\n\nThe snow melts into a patchwork landscape: a leafy forest edge, a stretch of sandy earth, and, just beyond, the cluttered corner of an old town.\n\nHe crouches beside a small cocoon dangling from a branch, its cloak woven from twigs and leaves.\n\n“This little one builds its cloak from wherever it last battled. Leaves in the forest, sand in the dunes—and when nothing else is available, even dust and scraps from our own trash.”\n\nHe straightens, adjusting his glasses.\n\n“Lose the cloak, and it simply builds another. It always seems to end up with one, no matter where it finds itself.”\n\n“There’s a lesson in that, I think. A trainer won’t always get to choose the ground beneath their feet…”\n\n“But they can always choose how they adapt to it.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":19,"part":null,"text":"Before any words could be exchanged, the ground suddenly falls away beneath you. You stand on a high, windswept cliff, clouds rolling past at eye level. Far below, the sea stretches to the horizon.\n\n“Since the very first page of our history, people have looked up in awe of the birds.”\n\nSomewhere overhead, wings beat against the wind.\n\n“Some of your very first battles on Route One were against Flying-type Pokémon. Few types are as common, as familiar… or as quietly underestimated. They’ve carried trainers from city to city and lifted travelers over mountains no one could climb.”\n\nHe pauses, his voice dropping.\n\n“But there are three that rise high above them all in the Kanto region.”\n\n“One is said to appear to travelers lost in icy mountains. The second descends from the thunderclouds amid enormous bolts of lightning. And the third sets the sky ablaze with every beat of its wings.”\n\nThe wind sharpens. The sky splits into a swirling rage of elements.\n\nThrough the howling wind, Professor Cypress continues: “The old legends carry a warning: disturb not the harmony of fire, ice, or lightning. When these titans clash, the whole world feels it.”\n\nCypress steadies his glasses against the gale, excitement and nerves fighting across his face.\n\n“Trainers… you are about to stand before your first legendary Pokémon.”\n\n“Hold on tight.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":19,"part":"b","text":"As the battle ends, the wind falls still. Something glints on the cliff’s edge.\n\nProfessor Cypress kneels and lifts it carefully: a smooth stone tablet, pale sky-blue, faintly warm despite the cold.\n\nHe turns it over in his hands, eyes wide.\n\n“Remarkable… I’ve never seen anything quite like it.”\n\nHe tucks it carefully into his coat.\n\n“I’ll need some time with this one.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":20,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress keeps one hand resting over the pocket where the stone tablet sits, as if to make sure it’s still there.\n\nThe cliffs fade. Cobblestones settle beneath your feet in the square of a small Unova town, where a crowd has gathered before a tall man in an ornate robe. Behind him stand rows of figures in matching knight-like uniforms.\n\n“Pokémon,” the man calls out, “deserve to be free.”\n\nCypress's face poorly masks the disdain spreading across it.\n\n“Ghetsis of Team Plasma. To the people of Unova, his message sounded noble: that trainers were holding Pokémon captive, and the kindest thing anyone could do was let them go.”\n\n“Some listened. Some released partners they’d traveled with for years.”\n\nHe watches the crowd for a long moment.\n\n“But Ghetsis never wanted Pokémon to be free. He wanted everyone else to give theirs up—so he would be the only one left to use their power.”\n\n“He even raised and brainwashed a boy to believe every word of it, then placed him on a throne as the face of the cause.”\n\nHis jaw tightens.\n\n“The most dangerous lies, trainers, are the ones aimed at our kindness.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":21,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress straightens his coat as the town square fades, and lets out a slow breath.\n\n“Let’s leave Ghetsis to the history books for a while.”\n\nSalt air rolls in. You stand on a rocky shoreline where waves break against dark volcanic stone and steam curls up from warm tide pools. Just up the hill, a small orchard sways in the breeze.\n\nCypress wanders over to the nearest tree and plucks a bright red apple, turning it over admiringly.\n\nThe apple blinks at him.\n\nHe very carefully places it back on the branch.\n\n“…Perhaps we’ll eat later.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":22,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress glances back at the orchard once more, as if half-expecting it to follow him.\n\nThe shoreline fades. Polished wooden floors stretch out beneath you, and a large banner hangs above a raised platform: Fighting Dojo. The air smells of sweat and determination.\n\n“Saffron City once had two gyms. This dojo was one of them—until it lost a match to its psychic neighbor, and its official title along with it.”\n\nHe smiles.\n\n“They never stopped training, though. To this day, challengers who prove themselves here may earn the respect of the dojo’s prized fighters.”\n\nA blur streaks across the rafters, gone before your eyes can follow it.\n\nCypress squints upward.\n\n“Speed has always been prized in a dojo. Some Pokémon are so fast they seem to vanish entirely…”\n\nA dry, hollow shell drifts quietly down from the beams and settles on the floor. It doesn’t move. It doesn’t breathe.\n\nBut it is watching you.\n\n“…and some leave something behind.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":23,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress pauses at the dojo doors, bowing politely to the empty room before stepping out.\n\nGray clouds hang low over a quiet country road. An owl hoots somewhere in the trees, and a small, tightly closed flower bud sits at the edge of the path, waiting for a sun that refuses to come out.\n\nAhead, a young trainer is already tapping their foot.\n\n“Ah. It seems someone has been waiting for a challenger.”\n\nCypress steps aside with a small flourish.\n\n“Don’t keep them waiting, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":24,"part":null,"text":"As the battle ends, the clouds finally part. At the roadside, the little flower bud stirs in the sunlight and slowly unfurls into bright pink petals.\n\nProfessor Cypress beams. “There it is. Patience usually pays off.”\n\nThe road gives way to a sun-warmed meadow alive with color. Butterflies drift between the blossoms, and the air hums—a low, steady buzz that seems to come from everywhere at once.\n\nCypress lowers his voice, as if afraid to disturb something.\n\n“Every hive has exactly one queen. She raises the colony within her own body, and with nothing more than a scent, she commands them all—to gather, to build, to defend.”\n\nHe nods toward a massive honeycomb tucked into an old tree, glowing gold in the light.\n\n“And her workers are fiercely loyal. They will risk everything to protect her.”\n\nThe buzzing grows louder.\n\n“So tread carefully, trainers. In this meadow, you are the intruders.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":25,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress shoos a stray Combee away from his ear and hurries the group along, laughing nervously.\n\n“Yes—well—let’s not overstay our welcome.”\n\nThe buzzing fades into silence as you stroll towards the entrance of a cave. Cool, damp air settles over you as the walls glitter with veins of crystal and gemstones that catch what little light there is.\n\nCypress’s voice drops to a whisper that echoes all around you.\n\n“You’ve all seen evolution by now. A Pokémon grows, changes, and becomes something new—permanently.”\n\n“But researchers have documented something rarer. Something that goes beyond evolution itself.”\n\nHe runs a hand along the glittering wall.\n\n“Certain fully evolved Pokémon, when the conditions are just right, can transform into something far more powerful. Their bodies change. Their abilities change. Some even take on an entirely different type.”\n\n“We call it Mega Evolution.”\n\nDeep in the dark, a pair of gemstones flicker to life… and the sound of steel scrapes against the stone.\n\nCypress swallows hard, though he can’t hide the grin spreading across his face.\n\n“Even one of these monsters can be a real handful for the most experienced trainer.”\n\n“Drop everything, you'll probably need both hands...”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":26,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress shakes out his hands, flexing his fingers as if checking that they’re all still there.\n\nWarm sea air washes over you once again. Palm trees sway against a bright blue sky, and the scent of flowers drifts in from the islands you’ve visited before.\n\n“Welcome back to Alola.”\n\nHe smiles.\n\n“These islands are a world all their own. Smoldering volcanoes, snowcapped peaks, and sunshine that never seems to end—all within sight of one another. Over many generations, familiar Pokémon have reshaped themselves to survive here. Eruptions drove some up into the mountains, where their bodies turned to ice and steel. Others were brought here by people, and became something new from the lives they were given.”\n\nHe pauses, eyes twinkling.\n\n“And in at least one case, researchers honestly have no idea what happened. The locals insist it ate too many pancakes.”\n\n“You’ll recognize many of the faces you’re about to meet. Just don’t assume you know what they’re capable of.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":27,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress shakes the sand from his shoes as the islands fade behind you.\n\nThe scene settles into a quiet, dimly lit room. The floor is lined with glowing panels, and the air hums with a pressure you can feel behind your eyes.\n\n“You may remember the dojo in Saffron City—and the neighbor it lost its title to.”\n\nHe glances around the room.\n\n“This is that neighbor.”\n\n“Psychic-type Pokémon have always unsettled people. Some read the future in the movement of the stars. Others stare at the sun from dawn to dusk, seeing what has already happened and what is still to come. And one is said to have a mind that outpaces any computer ever built.”\n\nOne of the glowing panels flickers beneath Cypress’s feet—and suddenly he’s standing on the other side of the room.\n\nHe blinks, straightens his glasses, and clears his throat.\n\n“…I’d suggest watching your step.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":28,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress steps carefully off the last glowing panel, as if testing the floor before trusting it.\n\nThe gym dissolves into a moonlit clearing high on a mountainside. Soft light pours over a garden of wildflowers, and somewhere nearby, small figures are dancing in a circle beneath the full moon.\n\nCypress’s voice softens to a hush.\n\n“Some Pokémon are so gentle, so charming, that for years researchers simply filed them away as ‘Normal.’”\n\nHe smiles.\n\n“It took us a remarkably long time to realize we’d been wrong. These Pokémon carry a magic all their own—one that even dragons have learned to fear.”\n\nA sweet perfume drifts across the clearing, followed faintly by the smell of fresh-baked bread.\n\nCypress sniffs the air, puzzled… then delighted.\n\n“Don’t let their charm fool you, trainers. Fairies have never been as harmless as they look.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":29,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress lingers at the edge of the clearing a moment longer, still sniffing the air for that bread.\n\nThe moonlight remains as the ground gives way beneath you—and suddenly you’re standing on the deck of a small wooden boat, rocking gently on a calm, endless sea. Dozens of other boats drift alongside, lashed together into a floating village. Lanterns glow. Somewhere, someone is singing.\n\n“Roughly four thousand years ago, there were people who never truly lived on land. They followed the currents from one sea to the next, and the ocean was their home.”\n\nHe gazes out over the water.\n\n“They’re remembered as the People of the Water. And above all else, they revered one small Pokémon.”\n\n“They called it the Prince of the Sea.”\n\n“It’s born on the cold seafloor, yet no matter how far it drifts, it always finds its way home. Wherever it swims, smaller companions are never far behind. And it’s said to share its heart with anyone it meets—person or Pokémon alike.”\n\nThe singing stops. Every lantern on the water turns toward the horizon, where a faint blue glow is rising beneath the waves.\n\nCypress removes his glasses, wipes the salt spray from them, and sets them back with care.\n\n“Their legends speak of a temple beneath the sea… and of the king who sank it there to keep it safe.”\n\n“Hold on to the railing, trainers. The sea is about to answer them.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":30,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress grips the boat’s railing a moment longer as the sea grows still, then steps onto solid ground with visible relief.\n\nBlack volcanic rock crunches beneath your feet. To one side, a mountain smolders, its peak wreathed in ash. To the other, waves crash against the cliffs. Between them, two groups face off—one dressed in red, the other in blue.\n\n“Team Magma and Team Aqua.”\n\nCypress folds his arms.\n\n“Magma believed humanity needed more land to grow—so they set out to create it. Aqua believed the sea was the source of all life—so they meant to return the world to it.”\n\n“Each was utterly certain it was saving the world. And each was willing to tear it apart to prove it.”\n\nThe red side raises a fist toward the mountain. The blue side answers with a roar toward the sea. The ground trembles beneath you.\n\nHis jaw tightens.\n\n“Two leaders. Two grand visions. And not one of them stopped to ask what the world itself wanted.”\n\n“Stand your ground, trainers. Today, you may be the only ones here who aren’t trying to reshape it.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":31,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress brushes a fine layer of ash from his coat sleeves, coughing lightly.\n\n“After all that fire and fury… I think we’ve earned something gentler.”\n\nThe volcanic rock gives way, and the world turns green. You stand at the edge of a vast flower field, warm and quiet. Rows of sunflowers turn slowly to follow the sun across the sky, and soft tufts of cotton drift past on the breeze.\n\nCypress breathes in deeply.\n\n“Grass-type Pokémon are often the first to return after disaster. Long after the fires burn out and the floods recede, they’re the ones that coax life back into the land.”\n\nHe crouches beside a bed of flowers, smiling.\n\n“Some even share their own strength to nurse others back to health.”\n\nHe rises, brushing off his knees.\n\n“But don’t mistake gentle for helpless. A garden that heals can also defend itself.”\n\n“Mind the flowers, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":32,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress plucks a stray petal from his hair, still humming as the flower field fades.\n\nA small town square settles into place around you, busy with travelers. Near the fountain, a trainer is already waiting, arms folded, a big shaggy dog-like Pokémon sitting patiently at their side.\n\nCypress strides forward to greet them—and stops abruptly, nose-first against something that isn’t there.\n\nHe steps back, rubbing his nose, and squints at the empty air.\n\n“…It seems this trainer’s team has already set up their defenses.”\n\nHe straightens his glasses with as much dignity as he can manage.\n\n“Your move, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":33,"part":null,"text":"Night falls all at once. You stand at the gate of an old cemetery on the edge of a quiet town. Mist curls between the headstones, and somewhere inside a forgotten cottage, a teacup rattles on its saucer.\n\nCypress’s voice drops low.\n\n“Many Ghost-type Pokémon aren’t born out of fear. They’re born out of feelings too strong to fade.”\n\nHe nods toward a headstone, where a pale, dog-like Pokémon lies curled asleep at its base.\n\n“Some are bound by love. A Pokémon mourned deeply enough may return—still loyal, still keeping watch over the one it lost.”\n\nHis gaze shifts to a battered doll lying in the grass. Its stitched mouth seems to twitch.\n\n“And others… are bound by grudges. Things thrown away and forgotten have a way of remembering.”\n\nA cold wind sweeps through the gate. Every lantern in the cemetery flickers at once.\n\nCypress pulls his coat a little tighter.\n\n“Be respectful in here, trainers. Not everyone resting in this place is ready to let go.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":34,"part":null,"text":"The cemetery fades, but the flickering doesn’t. The light in the cottage window stutters on and off… then the glow slips out through the glass and drifts toward you.\n\nProfessor Cypress follows it with his eyes, delighted.\n\n“Ah. So that’s what was playing with the lanterns.”\n\nThe scene shifts to a cluttered old house. A refrigerator hums in the corner. A washing machine sits beside a dusty microwave, an electric fan, and a lawn mower that has clearly seen better days.\n\n“Its body is made of plasma—something between a spirit and a spark of electricity. It slips into machines and makes them its own.”\n\nThe fan begins to spin on its own. The microwave beeps twice.\n\n“Researchers are still studying it. Some believe it could be the power source for an entirely new kind of motor.”\n\nEvery appliance in the room shudders to life at once.\n\nCypress takes a careful step back.\n\n“…They also say it’s terribly mischievous.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":35,"part":null,"text":"The hum of the appliances fades into the steady rhythm of waves. You stand on a windswept island, where the scent of salt mixes with sweat and effort. At the foot of the hills sits a sprawling dojo.\n\nProfessor Cypress straightens his coat, unusually solemn.\n\n“Every fighter must eventually choose what kind of fighter they want to be.”\n\nIn a clearing nearby, two small, bear-like Pokémon spar with fierce determination, each giving a firm tug on the tuft of fur atop its head before charging forward.\n\n“These two have trained side by side for a long time. Soon, each will climb a tower to master a single art. One teaches the strength to end a fight with a single, decisive blow. The other teaches the patience to flow like a river—sometimes rapid, sometimes calm.”\n\nHe watches them circle each other, a small smile forming.\n\n“The path they choose will shape everything they become.”\n\nAround the clearing, the dojo’s other students set down their training and turn toward you.\n\n“It seems the students would like a word with the newcomers first. Show them what you’ve learned, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":35,"part":"b","text":"As the last fighter bows out, the dojo falls quiet. Something glints on the training floor.\n\nProfessor Cypress kneels and lifts it carefully: a smooth stone tablet, deep rust-red, still warm, as if it had been part of the fight.\n\nHe turns it over in his hands, then reaches into his coat and sets the first plate next to the new one.\n\n“Two now…”\n\nHe lingers a moment, studying them side by side, before tucking both away.","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":36,"part":null,"text":"The salt air fades into the warm hush of a meadow at dusk. The sun sinks on one side of the sky while the moon rises on the other.\n\nA small brown Pokémon with an oversized cream collar bounds out of the tall grass. It shakes itself off, tilts its head, and gazes up at you with enormous, sparkling eyes.\n\nProfessor Cypress kneels slowly, already melting.\n\n“Few Pokémon have puzzled researchers quite like this one. Its genetic code is irregular—unstable, even.”\n\nHe offers a hand. The little Pokémon sniffs it, sneezes, then nuzzles its head into his palm with a happy little \"vee\"!\n\n“Water, fire, lightning, ice, a forest’s moss, the light of the sun or the moon… even simple affection. Change what surrounds it, and it changes to match.”\n\nIt gives his fingers one last nuzzle, then bounds off toward the treeline, ears flopping with every hop. At the edge of the meadow, eight shapes wait in the half-light. Each one is unmistakably related, and each one is entirely different.\n\nCypress rises, smiling.\n\n“Eight paths, one beginning. Bring every bit of versatility you have, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":37,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress watches the meadow fade with a wistful little sigh.\n\nA wide, clear lake stretches out before you, so vast its far shore is little more than a blur. Gentle waves lap at the rocks, and a dense stand of trees crowds the water’s edge. Propped against a boulder, half-sunk in the wet sand, is an old fishing rod—as if someone stepped away and never came back for it.\n\nCypress picks it up, turning it over with a fond smile.\n\n“Every trainer remembers their first rod. Not because it was any good—because of what it taught them. Patience.”\n\nHe casts the line out over the water and settles onto a flat rock at the water’s edge.\n\n“Take your time, trainers. You never know what’s waiting just below the surface.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":38,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress reels in his line one last time and props the old rod back against the boulder, right where he found it.\n\nClouds roll in over the lake, and a steady rain begins to fall. You follow the shoreline around a bend, where the rising water has crept over the path, leaving only a narrow strip of mud between the lake and the trees.\n\nHalfway along it, a trainer leans against a tree trunk with their hood up, as if waiting out the weather—or waiting for someone to pass.\n\nThey push off the tree and step into the middle of the path.\n\nCypress sighs, turning up his collar against the rain.\n\n“There’s no going around this one, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":39,"part":null,"text":"After your battle, the rain thickens. With every step along the shore, the clouds hang lower and darker, until thunder rolls across the water.\n\nThen the lake begins to churn.\n\nProfessor Cypress stops at the water’s edge, his voice barely carrying over the storm.\n\n“Locals have always said this lake was born in a storm—that long ago, a frenzy of furious Pokémon tore the land open, and the rain filled what they left behind.”\n\nOn the water near the shore, dozens of small orange fish flop aimlessly at the surface.\n\n“The weakest Pokémon in the world. Or so everyone believed.”\n\nA low, electric hum pulses through the air—coming from somewhere beyond the trees, too steady to be the storm.\n\n“But someone discovered that the right signal, broadcast in just the wrong way, could force them to change.”\n\nOne by one, the fish begin to glow. Their bodies stretch and twist into long, serpentine shapes, roaring as they burst from the water.\n\nAt the center of the lake, one rises higher than all the rest, towering over the waves.\n\nCypress steadies himself against a rock, rain streaming down his glasses.\n\n“The stories say that on a day like this, one of them rose from these waters with scales burning red. That one has long since become legend…”\n\nHe watches the towering shape thrash against the storm.\n\n“…but this one is every bit as furious. Once its rage begins, it never settles until everything around it lies in ruins.”\n\n“Trainers… this is one storm you’ll have to stand in.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":39,"part":"b","text":"When the last roar fades, the clouds finally break. Sunlight spills across the lake, and the water stills into a perfect mirror.\n\nSomething glints in the shallows at Cypress’s feet.\n\nHe wades in up to his ankles and lifts it out: a smooth stone tablet, deep ocean blue, cool and dripping.\n\nCypress studies the tablet for a long, quiet moment.\n\n“Three…”\n\nHe tucks it away carefully, shaking the water from his shoes.","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":40,"part":null,"text":"The calm of the lake gives way to the glow of city lights. You stand on a crowded boulevard beneath a towering spire, the air full of clinking café cups and passing conversation.\n\nProfessor Cypress nods toward a stylish café on the corner, its windows glowing a deep, warm red.\n\n“Lysandre. Brilliant inventor. Generous philanthropist. Descended from Kalos royalty and admired by nearly everyone who knew his name.”\n\n“For years, he gave his fortune away trying to make the world a better place. And when it didn’t change fast enough, he decided the people were the problem.”\n\n“He came to believe there simply wasn’t enough to go around—that people would always fight over what little there was. So he decided who deserved to live in his ‘beautiful’ world.”\n\nCypress’s jaw tightens.\n\n“Everyone else, he meant to erase.”\n\n“And he found the means to do it. Buried deep beneath Kalos lies a weapon of unimaginable power, capable of wiping out nearly every living thing. Lysandre intends to fire it.”\n\nA low rumble shakes the cobblestones. Far beyond the city, something ancient stirs beneath the earth.\n\n“The most dangerous villains are the ones certain they’re the hero. Stop him, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":40,"part":"b","text":"Where the last flames of the battle flicker out, something glints among the embers.\n\nProfessor Cypress brushes away the ash and lifts it free: a smooth stone tablet, deep ember red, still radiating heat.\n\nHe holds it a moment longer than he needs to, then slips it into his coat.\n\n“Four.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":41,"part":null,"text":"The city lights flicker out, replaced by the deep green hush of an untouched jungle. Vines hang from towering trees, and the air is thick with the calls of unseen Pokémon.\n\nProfessor Cypress lowers his voice to a whisper.\n\n“Today, nearly every trainer carries a Poké Ball without a second thought. But there was a time when they were brand new—and not everyone welcomed them.”\n\nHe parts a curtain of vines, revealing a narrow trail.\n\n“As the first Poké Balls spread, more and more people ventured into places like this, hoping to fill them. Many of the Pokémon here had never seen a human before. They had no idea what those little spheres meant.”\n\nSomewhere above you, branches creak under something heavy, swinging swiftly from tree to tree.\n\n“But some of them learned quickly. Deep in these jungles lived fierce, clever Pokémon that roamed in packs and answered to no one. They came to see the people carrying Poké Balls as a threat—and they took it upon themselves to protect every creature in their forest.”\n\nA long vine drops into the path ahead and snaps taut.\n\nCypress raises his hands slowly.\n\n“To them, trainers, we’re the ones who don’t belong here.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":42,"part":null,"text":"The jungle’s humid air gives way to dry heat. You stand in a bustling desert market at the edge of a shimmering oasis, its stalls piled high with spices, silks, and glittering trinkets.\n\nProfessor Cypress pats his coat pockets out of habit, then relaxes.\n\n“Markets like this have always attracted tricksters. Pickpockets, illusionists, sneaks who can blend right into the scenery…”\n\nA vendor shouts. Half her stall has simply vanished.\n\n“…but none of them compare to the legend of this place.”\n\nHe gestures toward the oasis.\n\n“It’s said a mischievous Pokémon could open golden rings that reached anywhere in the world. Anything it fancied—treasure, trinkets, even an entire castle—it simply pulled through and hid away. In the end, it caused so much chaos that its power had to be sealed inside a bottle.”\n\nA small golden ring blinks open beside Cypress’s head. A tiny hand reaches out, plucks the glasses right off his face, and vanishes.\n\nHe squints at the empty air.\n\n“…Keep a close eye on your belongings, trainers. And mine, if you see them.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":43,"part":null,"text":"The desert heat melts into a cool, quiet afternoon. A country road winds through rolling green hills—and somewhere nearby, something is snoring.\n\nLoudly.\n\nProfessor Cypress, a spare pair of glasses perched slightly crooked on his nose, follows the sound around a bend and stops short.\n\nAn enormous Pokémon lies sprawled across the entire road, its belly rising and falling like a small hill. Around it, the grass is littered with berry pits, empty milk pails, and the crumbs of what was once a very large cake.\n\n“Ah. A road-block.”\n\nHe steps carefully around a smear of frosting.\n\n“Normal-type Pokémon are so familiar, we forget how remarkable they are. This one, for example, won’t feel full until it’s eaten nearly nine hundred pounds of food in a day… and then it sleeps through almost anything.”\n\nThe snoring stops. One heavy eyelid cracks open.\n\n“…Almost.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":43,"part":"b","text":"As the dust settles, the road falls quiet again. Pressed into the flattened grass where the great Pokémon lay sits something pale and smooth.\n\nCypress picks up the stone tablet, plain and white as fresh snow, and brushes the crumbs from its surface.\n\n“Five.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":44,"part":null,"text":"The road fades, and familiar city lights rise around you once more—the same boulevards, the same towering spire. But something has changed. Cranes loom over half-built streets, and an uneasy hum hangs in the air.\n\nProfessor Cypress looks up at the spire, frowning.\n\n“Kalos, a few years after Lysandre’s defeat. His weapon was stopped—but not before it left a wound. Deep beneath this city, something old was jolted awake that never should have been.”\n\nA Pokémon bolts across the street. Its body flares with unnatural light, transforming before your eyes with no trainer anywhere in sight.\n\n“The land has been struggling to heal ever since.”\n\nHe crouches beside a shallow, murky puddle, where a small, round Pokémon blinks up at him lazily.\n\n“Nature is always reshaping its creatures. In a distant region, some of these little ones lost their place in the water and were driven onto land. Over generations, their bodies grew a poisonous film to protect them—and they became something entirely new.”\n\nThe ground trembles. Cracks race across the pavement, and from them, glowing green cells begin to rise and swirl together.\n\n“But when the balance is truly broken, nature doesn’t wait for generations. It sends its guardian.”\n\nSomething immense takes shape above the spire.\n\n“Trainers… the earth itself has come to set things right.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":44,"part":"b","text":"As the tremors fade, the cracks in the pavement slowly close. In the dust where the ground had split, something glints.\n\nProfessor Cypress lifts it out: a smooth stone tablet, the color of rich, dark earth.\n\nHe turns it over slowly. This time, he doesn’t put it away.\n\n“Six. I’ve spent every spare moment since the first one trying to understand them. No markings I can read. No material I can identify. They’re older than any record I’ve found—perhaps older than the places we found them.”\n\nHe glances at Mew.\n\n“And Mew hasn’t given me the slightest sign that they’re part of the simulation. As far as I can tell… they’re simply here. As real as you or me.”\n\nHe wraps the tablet carefully in a cloth and tucks it away, giving the pocket a reassuring pat.\n\n“I’ll make sense of them. Eventually.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":45,"part":null,"text":"The city fades, and with it, every sign of modern life. Cold wind howls across a wild, untamed land. Snow dusts the distant peaks, and at the center of it all rises a towering mountain, its summit lost in swirling clouds.\n\nProfessor Cypress pulls his coat tighter, his breath fogging in the air.\n\n“We’ve traveled a long way back this time. Before Sinnoh had its name, this land was called Hisui.”\n\nHe gestures toward a small cluster of wooden buildings far below, smoke curling from their chimneys.\n\n“Settlers came here from across the sea to build a new life. But the land was harsh, and its Pokémon were wild in a way we can hardly imagine today. Most people were terrified of them. The Poké Ball itself had only just been invented.”\n\nSomewhere in the snow, a pale, ghostly shape slips between the trees.\n\n“Pokémon you think you know looked very different here. The bitter cold, the rugged land—even the energy of that sacred mountain—shaped them into something fiercer. Something stranger.”\n\nThe wind carries a distant, eerie cry.\n\nCypress adjusts his spare glasses, a glint of excitement in his eyes.\n\n“Familiar names. Unfamiliar faces. Stay sharp, trainers—Hisui doesn’t forgive carelessness.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":46,"part":null,"text":"A sharp gust sweeps snow across the slopes. Somewhere ahead, hooves thunder over the frozen ground.\n\nA great antlered Pokémon bounds past at breathtaking speed, a rider clinging to its back. High above, a broad-winged bird glides over the peaks, another rider tucked between its shoulders.\n\nProfessor Cypress watches them go, wide-eyed.\n\n“In a land where most people feared Pokémon, a rare few earned something extraordinary: their trust.”\n\nHe points across the landscape—a hulking bear lumbering through the marsh, a nimble climber scaling a sheer cliff face, a shimmering fish cutting through the icy surf.\n\n“The clans of Hisui revered these Pokémon. They didn’t carry just anyone. They chose their riders—and only those they found worthy.”\n\nHis voice softens with something close to envy.\n\n“Long before Poké Balls came to Hisui, this was how its people and Pokémon learned to rely on one another.”\n\nThe antlered Pokémon wheels around, its rider gone, and lowers its head toward you.\n\nCypress swallows.\n\n“It seems they’d like to see if you’re worthy, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":47,"part":null,"text":"The snowy peaks of Hisui melt away, and the air turns thick and warm. Towering ferns sway beneath an orange sky. Strange cries echo across a primordial landscape where shelled creatures crawl through the shallows and winged shadows circle overhead.\n\nProfessor Cypress gazes around in reverent silence.\n\n“Long before the first human ever set foot here, the world belonged to them. Ancient Pokémon—armored, toothed, and winged—ruled the land, the sea, and the sky.”\n\nHe kneels beside a tide pool, where a spiral-shelled Pokémon slowly drags itself across the rocks.\n\n“Today, all we have left are their fossils. We’ve even learned to revive a few of them. But for a long time, no one could explain why they all disappeared.”\n\nHe looks up. High in the sky, a bright point of light grows larger… and larger.\n\n“Some researchers believe the answer came from the stars—a great meteor that struck the earth and changed the world forever.”\n\nThe light burns brighter, trailing fire across the sky. The ancient Pokémon fall silent.\n\nCypress’s voice lowers.\n\n“And a few believe that meteor wasn’t empty. That something was sleeping inside it… something that would wait a very, very long time to wake.”\n\nHe shakes his head and turns back to you.\n\n“But that’s a story for another day. Right now, these giants are still very much alive—and they are as mean as they look.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":47,"part":"b","text":"As the dust settles across the ancient landscape, something glints in the cracked earth: a smooth stone tablet, the warm, mottled tan of sun-baked rock.\n\nCypress lifts it gently, as though it might crumble.\n\n“Seven. And somehow… this one feels older than the fossils around it.”\n\nHe wraps it carefully and tucks it away.","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":48,"part":null,"text":"The ancient world fades, and the cold winds of Hisui return. But something is wrong. Above the sacred mountain, the clouds have split open into a jagged, glowing wound in the sky.\n\nProfessor Cypress stares up at it, unsettled.\n\n“We’re back in Hisui. The people here revered a handful of extraordinary Pokémon as their protectors—the nobles. The Lord of the Woods. The Lady of the Ridge. The Lord of the Isles, the Lord of the Hollow, and the Lord of the Tundra.”\n\nA crack of strange lightning splits the air and strikes the forest below. A furious roar answers it.\n\n“Each one had its own warden, was honored by the clans, and was trusted to keep the land in balance. But when that light began falling from the sky, the nobles were struck—and driven into a frenzy.”\n\nTrees crash in the distance. Something massive is coming.\n\nCypress’s voice tightens.\n\n“They aren’t evil, trainers. They’re frightened, and they’re hurting. The people of Hisui calmed them with offerings of their favorite foods… but first, someone had to be brave enough to stand in their way.”\n\n“Let’s give them back their peace.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":49,"part":null,"text":"The forest falls quiet as the frenzy fades. Above the sacred mountain, the wound in the sky still glows—wider now, pulsing as if it were breathing.\n\nProfessor Cypress follows your gaze upward.\n\n“Calming the nobles was only half the answer. Someone opened that tear in the sky. And for a long time, no one suspected who.”\n\nA cheerful voice drifts down the trail. A traveling merchant appears: golden hair, an easy smile, a pack of wares slung over one shoulder. It’s the kind of face everyone in Hisui trusted.\n\n“Volo. He traded goods across Hisui, offered travelers advice, and always seemed delighted to help.”\n\nCypress’s expression hardens.\n\n“But that smile was a mask. Volo made a pact with a Pokémon that had been banished from our world for its violence—cast out to a strange, distorted realm on the other side. Together, they tore open the sky.”\n\nThe merchant’s smile never falters. Behind him, the air twists and warps, and a vast, shadowy shape begins to unfurl.\n\n“He wanted power. Enough to unmake this world entirely—and build a new one in its place.”\n\nCypress steps in front of the group.\n\n“Some villains wear armor. Others wear a friendly face. Be ready, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":50,"part":null,"text":"The tear in the sky closes behind you, and heavy clouds roll in. You stand in a field of ripening grain at the edge of a quiet farming village in Unova. The air smells of rain that hasn’t yet fallen.\n\nProfessor Cypress squints at the darkening sky.\n\n“Weather has always shaped the lives of the people who depend on it. A good season means a full harvest. A bad one…”\n\nA bolt of lightning splits the sky and strikes a distant tree, setting it ablaze. Seconds later, a howling wind tears across the fields, flattening the crops.\n\n“…can mean ruin.”\n\nTwo figures streak through the clouds—one crackling with electricity, the other trailing a roaring gale.\n\n“The people of Unova told stories of two spirits of storm and lightning that flew wherever they pleased, blowing houses away and setting forests ablaze. They were feared and disliked everywhere they went.”\n\nHe turns toward the hills, where the clouds are just beginning to part.\n\n“But the same stories speak of a third—a guardian of the fields who brought bountiful harvests, and who would arrive whenever the other two clashed to put an end to their quarrel.”\n\nA small, round Pokémon peeks nervously out from beneath a barn’s eaves, its body shifting shape as the weather changes around it.\n\nCypress smiles faintly.\n\n“Until the guardian arrives, I suppose we’ll have to weather this storm ourselves.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":51,"part":null,"text":"The storm clouds don’t break. Instead, they follow you, thickening and darkening, until the farmland gives way to black rock and a towering volcano, its mouth glowing red against the night.\n\nProfessor Cypress has to shout over the thunder.\n\n“There are places where fire and storm meet. Ash rises from the mountain, the clouds grow heavy with static, and the sky above the volcano fills with lightning.”\n\nThe ground shudders. Lava spills over the rim, lighting the clouds from below.\n\n“There’s an old account of one such night. The mountain erupted. Lightning struck the crater. The explosion could be seen for miles.”\n\nA searing bolt slams into the peak. The world turns white. When your vision clears, something is standing at the crater’s edge—small, lean, crackling with electricity, its fur standing on end.\n\n“And when the smoke cleared, something walked out of the fire that should never have survived it.”\n\nThe figure tilts its head toward you. Sparks dance along its claws.\n\nCypress smirks.\n\n“They say it moves as fast as lightning itself. Don’t blink, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":51,"part":"b","text":"As the last sparks fade, the storm finally rolls away, leaving the volcano quiet beneath a starry sky. In the cooling ash at the crater’s edge, something glows faintly.\n\nCypress lifts it carefully: a smooth stone tablet, bright electric yellow, still humming with static.\n\nHe winces as a tiny spark snaps against his fingers, then wraps it and tucks it away.\n\n“Eight. And this one bites.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":52,"part":null,"text":"The volcano’s glow fades into the soft light of a temple town at dusk. Two great towers rise above the rooftops, their tiers stacked high against the evening sky.\n\nProfessor Cypress gazes up at them, reverent.\n\n“Ecruteak, long ago. Two towers stood here, each a sacred home. A great silver guardian of the sea once perched atop this one…”\n\nHe turns to the second tower, gleaming in the last of the sunlight.\n\n“…and a bird of rainbow flame watched over the town from the other.”\n\nThunder rumbles. A bolt of lightning strikes the first tower, and within moments, flames are racing up its walls.\n\nThrough the smoke, a vast silver shape lifts from the tower’s peak, cries out once, and flies toward the sea, never to return.\n\nCypress’s voice drops.\n\n“Three young Eevee were trapped inside. No one could reach them in time.”\n\nThe skies finally open, and a sudden downpour drowns the flames. Then, from the other tower, a brilliant light descends, trailing feathers of every color, and settles over the smoldering ruins.\n\n“The bird of rainbow flame swept down and breathed new life into them. Three new beasts rose from the ashes: one of lightning, one of fire, and one of pure water.”\n\n“But the people of Ecruteak didn’t see a miracle. They saw strange, unnatural, and powerful creatures emerging from the ruins. They were afraid. They drove them away.”\n\nThree shapes burst from the smoldering tower and race straight toward you.\n\n“They’ve been running ever since. Let’s give them a reason to stop, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":53,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress lets out a long, slow breath as the smoke of Ecruteak drifts away.\n\nMorning sunlight spills over a quiet farmstead. Fences stretch across green pastures, a weathered barn leans comfortably against the hillside, and somewhere nearby, a dog barks happily at nothing in particular.\n\nCypress leans on the fence and closes his eyes as the breeze passes.\n\n“After a story like that, it’s good to remember that most days, for most Pokémon, are simply… days. Sunlight. Grass. A good meal.”\n\nHe opens one eye and smiles.\n\n“Go on, trainers. Enjoy the quiet while it lasts.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":54,"part":null,"text":"The peaceful farmland gives way to wide, sun-baked plains. In the distance, the ground trembles beneath the footsteps of something enormous.\n\nProfessor Cypress shields his eyes against the glare, watching a massive shape lumber across the horizon.\n\n“Paldea. Hidden across this region grows a rare and remarkable herb—one said to restore health the instant it’s eaten.”\n\n“But the Pokémon here found it first.”\n\nA shadow sweeps over you. High above, a bird the size of a small house circles lazily, then drops something heavy that crashes into the rocks nearby.\n\n“Ordinary Pokémon ate their fill of the herb and grew to astonishing size. Crabs as big as boulders. Serpents of living steel. Beasts that shake the earth with every step. The locals call them Titans.”\n\nHe pauses, his voice softening.\n\n“Not long ago, a young man set out to find the herb for himself. His closest partner had been badly hurt, and nothing else could heal it. The only way to reach the herb… was through the Titans guarding it.”\n\nThe ground rumbles again, louder this time.\n\nCypress tightens the strap on his satchel and grins.\n\n“They may not be legends, trainers, but they’re certainly legendary in size. Let’s help him get there.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":55,"part":null,"text":"The sun-baked plains fall away beneath your feet. You descend into a vast crater hidden at the heart of Paldea, where mist curls around crystal formations and the air hums with a strange energy.\n\nProfessor Cypress looks around in wonder—and unease.\n\n“Area Zero. Few places in the world are as mysterious. And at the very bottom, a brilliant professor once built something that should never have existed.”\n\nAt the base of the crater, a sleek laboratory glows against the rock.\n\n“A machine that reached across time. Pokémon from the distant past and the far-off future were pulled into the present, and this crater filled with creatures that had no business living side by side.”\n\nA sharp cry echoes off the walls. Two powerful, lizard-like Pokémon circle one another—one bristling with ancient ferocity, the other gleaming with futuristic light.\n\n“The professor’s own life was lost to that chaos. But the machine kept running. A copy of the professor’s mind was left behind to protect it—and even though that copy knew the machine had to be stopped, it was built to defend it at any cost.”\n\nCypress shakes his head slowly.\n\n“The professor dreamed of a world where past and future could meet. But some doors are better left closed.”\n\nHe turns to you, resolve in his eyes.\n\n“Let’s shut it down, trainers. For good.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":56,"part":null,"text":"The crater fades into the cool green of a mountain village. Paper lanterns hang between wooden houses, and in the village square, three carved statues stand proudly atop a stone monument.\n\nProfessor Cypress studies them with a skeptical eye.\n\n“Kitakami. For generations, its people have honored these three as heroes—the brave Pokémon who saved the village from a terrible ogre.”\n\nHe turns away from the statues, toward a dark mountain looming over the village.\n\n“But history is usually written by the victors, or from an incomplete perspective.”\n\nThe scene shifts. Deep in the mountain, a small, mask-wearing Pokémon dances happily beside an old man, the two of them laughing together in a firelit cave.\n\n“There was an ‘ogre,’ yes. A gentle Pokémon who lived with a single human companion and a collection of treasured masks. Then three Pokémon came in the night and stole them.”\n\nThe cave falls dark. The old man is gone. Only a single teal mask remains.\n\n“When the ogre came to the village to take its masks back, the people saw only a monster. And the thieves? They became heroes.”\n\nCypress’s jaw tightens.\n\n“Even the thieves weren’t acting alone. Something small and sweet-smelling had them on a leash—poisoned their minds with a treat and sent them to do its dirty work.”\n\nA faint scent of sugar and rot drifts through the air.\n\n“Today, trainers, we set the record straight.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":56,"part":"b","text":"When the last chain shatters, a heavy silence falls over the village. Something glints at the foot of the monument, half-hidden in the shadow of the three statues.\n\nCypress picks it up: a smooth stone tablet, a deep, sickly violet.\n\nHe glances back at the statues, then at the tablet in his hand.\n\n“Nine.”\n\nHe tucks it away without another word.","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":57,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress breathes in the mountain air, letting the weight of Kitakami settle behind him.\n\nA warm breeze rolls across an open hillside dotted with wildflowers. Down the path, a young man strolls along with a sandwich in one hand, a large, gruff-looking dog Pokémon trotting proudly at his side—tail wagging, no worse for wear.\n\nCypress watches them go, a small smile tugging at his face.\n\n“Looks like someone found what they were looking for.”\n\nHe turns back toward the open hills, where the tall grass rustles with movement.\n\n“Let’s see what else is out here, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":58,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress pauses at the edge of the hill, looking out toward the horizon, where the land gives way to the sea.\n\nThe breeze turns salty. You follow a winding path down to a rocky beach, where the tide churns restlessly around a cluster of small islands offshore. Out on the water, slow whirlpools spin and vanish, spin and vanish.\n\nNear the shoreline, a trainer sits on an overturned rowboat, tossing a Poké Ball idly from hand to hand. They look up as you approach and grin.\n\nCypress raises an eyebrow.\n\n“I don’t think they’re offering us a ride, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":59,"part":null,"text":"The beach fades, and the small boat carries you out across the churning water. Whirlpools spin all around, yet the hull glides between them as if guided by an unseen hand. Ahead, four small islands rise from the waves.\n\nProfessor Cypress grips the side of the boat, eyes on the horizon.\n\n“You’ll remember the silver guardian that fled the burning tower in Ecruteak. It flew toward the sea that night and never returned.”\n\nHe gestures toward the islands.\n\n“This is where it came. Some say these four islands were once one, until they were torn apart—and the guardian vanished into the deep beneath them.”\n\nThe boat drifts into a cave mouth. The sound of rushing water grows louder, echoing through dark, winding tunnels until they open into a vast underground basin, where a great waterfall pours into black water.\n\nCypress lowers his voice.\n\n“Its power is so immense that a single beat of its wings could tear a house apart. Some believe it could summon a storm that would rage for forty days. So it chose to hide here, deep beneath the sea, where it couldn’t harm anyone.”\n\nThe water begins to stir.\n\n“Sailors call it the guardian of the seas. Legend says it can now calm any storm—even the clash of the three great birds you faced long ago.”\n\nA low, haunting cry rises from the depths. The surface of the basin swells, then erupts.\n\nCypress steadies himself amid the ripples, awe written across his face.\n\n“It has been alone down here for a very long time, trainers. Show it the respect it deserves.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":60,"part":null,"text":"The cave’s damp chill gives way to warm, tropical air. You stand on a moonlit beach in Alola, palm trees swaying gently in the breeze.\n\nThen the sky tears open.\n\nA crackling funnel-shaped hole splits the night above the water, rimmed with shimmering, otherworldly light. Through it, you glimpse a strange place—swirling colors, impossible shapes, no sky and no ground.\n\nProfessor Cypress stares upward, utterly still.\n\n“An Ultra Wormhole. A doorway to a world beyond our own.”\n\nSomething drops from the opening and lands on the sand with a heavy thud. It doesn’t look like any Pokémon you’ve ever seen.\n\n“The creatures that come through these are called Ultra Beasts. They aren’t from here—not from this region, not from this world. And they’re every bit as lost and frightened as we would be in their place.”\n\nThe creature turns toward you, brimming with energy. More shapes follow it through the wormhole: one bulging with impossible muscle, another tall and gleaming like a rocket, another with an endless, gaping maw.\n\n“Lost, frightened… and immensely powerful. The energy of the wormhole only makes them stronger.”\n\nCypress plants his feet in the sand.\n\n“They’re looking for a way home, trainers. Until they find it, we’ll have to hold the line.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":61,"part":null,"text":"The wormhole seals itself shut, and the beach fades away.\n\nDawn breaks slowly over a quiet river valley. Mist hangs low over the water, birds chirp in the reeds, and beyond them the only sound is the gentle gurgle of the current.\n\nProfessor Cypress settles onto a fallen log, pours himself a cup of tea from a battered thermos, and cradles it in both hands.\n\n“Creatures from other worlds are thrilling, of course. But I’ll admit… there’s something to be said for a Pokémon that’s simply from here.”\n\nHe takes a long sip and waves lazily toward the riverbank.\n\n“Go on, trainers. I’ll be right here.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":62,"part":null,"text":"Professor Cypress drains the last of his tea, screws the cap back onto the thermos, and stands with a contented stretch.\n\nThe quiet valley gives way to a roar. You’re standing at the mouth of a tunnel, and beyond it lies a vast stadium packed with cheering fans. Banners wave from the stands. Lights blaze down onto a pristine pitch.\n\nAt the far end, a trainer strides out to meet you, waving to the crowd as if they’ve done this a hundred times.\n\nCypress leans in, raising his voice over the noise.\n\n“Some regions treat battling like a sport—and their trainers like stars.”\n\nHe grins and gives you a gentle nudge toward the field.\n\n“Don’t keep your fans waiting, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":63,"part":null,"text":"The stadium’s roar fades, though the crowd’s energy seems to linger in the air. Beyond the streets of a bustling city, green hills roll toward the horizon, lined with stone walls and smoking chimneys.\n\nProfessor Cypress tips his head toward the skyline.\n\n“Galar has a long, proud history—and its Pokémon carry the marks of it.”\n\nHe gestures toward the coast.\n\n“Centuries ago, fierce seafaring people sailed these waters. The Pokémon who lived and fought beside them grew so tough that parts of their bodies turned to iron.”\n\nThen toward the chimneys, where a strange, top-hatted Pokémon drifts lazily through the haze.\n\n“Later came the factories, and the air grew thick with smoke. Some Pokémon learned to breathe it in… and breathe out clean air in its place.”\n\nHis voice softens as he looks out over the sea.\n\n“Not every change was kind. Long ago, the climate shifted suddenly, and an ancient coral Pokémon was wiped out entirely. But it never truly disappeared. It lingers still—pale and restless.”\n\nSomewhere nearby, someone is tap-dancing across a frozen patch of pavement.\n\nCypress blinks, then chuckles.\n\n“Not every story here is a sad one. Stay sharp, trainers—Galar’s Pokémon have learned to be tough.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":64,"part":null,"text":"The city fades into a frozen expanse of snow and ice. Far to the north, at the crest of a lonely hill, a colossal tree stretches its branches toward a pale sky, heavy with strange, glowing fruit.\n\nProfessor Cypress pulls his coat tight against the wind.\n\n“You’ll remember the three legendary birds of Kanto—ice, lightning, and fire. Here in Galar, the legends tell of three as well. But they took a very different path.”\n\nA sharp crack echoes across the tundra. Something streaks over the snowfields on foot, kicking up great sprays of white—running, not flying.\n\n“One of them hardly bothers with flight at all. It races across the mountains on legs strong enough to crush stone.”\n\nA cold shimmer passes overhead. A slender, elegant bird glides by without a single beat of its wings, staring down with eyes that seem to see straight through you.\n\n“Another is held aloft by the power of its mind alone—graceful, beautiful… and utterly heartless.”\n\nThe shadows beneath the tree deepen. A dark, smoldering aura spills across the snow.\n\n“And the last carries a sinister flame that burns away the spirit of anything it touches.”\n\nCypress looks up at the great tree, its fruit glowing softly.\n\n“Once every few decades, all three return here to feast. Today, it seems, is one of those days.”\n\nHe steps forward, eyes bright.\n\n“Same names, trainers. Very different birds.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":65,"part":null,"text":"The great tree falls behind you. The path winds down into a quiet village, where a crumbling stone statue stands half-buried in snow. It shows a small, regal figure with a head shaped like a bulb.\n\nProfessor Cypress brushes the frost from its base.\n\n“Long ago, a king ruled Galar. He wasn’t a person. He was a Pokémon: merciful, gentle, the King of Bountiful Harvests. Wherever he walked, crops flourished.”\n\nHe smiles sadly at the weathered stone.\n\n“But a king like this draws his strength from the faith of his people. Over the centuries they forgot him. And his loyal steed, the mount he rode into every legend, left him behind.”\n\nTwo sets of tracks lead away from the statue.\n\nThe first is deep and heavy, cracking the ice wherever it lands.\n\n“One steed is a brute with a mask of ice harder than diamond. It takes whatever it wants by force.”\n\nThe second set is so faint it almost isn’t there.\n\n“The other runs silent through the night and never uses its eyes. Legend says its kick can separate a soul from its body.”\n\nCypress straightens his spare glasses and looks from one trail to the other.\n\n“You can’t follow both, trainers. Pick a path. The king waits for whichever steed comes home.”\n\nHe pulls a pair of odd carrots from his coat, one pale blue and one deep black, and grins.\n\n“I’m told they’re quite fond of these.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":66,"part":null,"text":"The tundra wind dies down. Snow falls in fat, lazy flakes, and the world goes quiet the way only deep winter can.\n\nProfessor Cypress sits on a low stone wall, blowing warmth into his fingers. He’s watching the treeline, where a pale figure seems to drift between the trunks. It’s there one moment and gone the next.\n\n“Probably just the snow,” he murmurs. He doesn’t sound convinced.\n\nHe tucks his hands into his sleeves and nods you onward.\n\n“Keep your scarves on, trainers. Whatever’s out there, it’s patient.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":67,"part":null,"text":"The snowfall thins, then stops altogether. Warm sunlight spills over you, and you’re standing on a grassy ridge high above the clouds.\n\nBelow the ridge yawns an enormous crater, so vast and deep that its floor vanishes into a sea of mist. A tall wall and a gated tower ring its edge. The gate is shut tight.\n\nA few paces down the path, a student trainer with a backpack stuffed full of maps is peering over the edge. They turn at your footsteps, notice the Poké Balls on your belts, and their eyes light up.\n\nProfessor Cypress unwinds his scarf and chuckles.\n\n“Students here are sent off across the whole region to find their own treasure. Looks like this one has decided it’s a battle with you, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":68,"part":null,"text":"The student waves goodbye, and the great gate grinds open. A long, winding path carries you down through the mist and into the crater itself.\n\nThe air turns warm and heavy. Ferns taller than houses crowd the slopes, and strange crystals glitter in the cliffs.\n\nProfessor Cypress pulls a battered red book from his coat.\n\n“An explorer reached the bottom of this crater two hundred years ago. Nobody believed a word he wrote.”\n\nHe leafs through the pages.\n\n“Long ago, Paldea’s kings were obsessed with treasure. One of them bought four ancient relics from a faraway land: a set of tablets, a sword, a vessel, and a string of beads. His greed seeped into them until they woke up as something dreadful. Their rampage nearly wiped out the kingdom, and they were sealed away.”\n\nHe taps the page.\n\n“According to this book, the land never recovered. The ground caved in and left this crater behind. Something precious was nearly lost with it: a creature that glittered like a jewel. The kings would hunt for it for centuries.”\n\nA howl rolls out of the ferns. A shape bursts into view. It’s a Pokémon you almost recognize, but wilder, shaggier and ancient, as if it had clawed its way out of prehistory.\n\nCypress snaps the book shut.\n\n“He wrote about these, too. Creatures from the distant past.”\n\nHe adjusts his glasses with a nervous smile.\n\n“Let’s find out how much of this book is true.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":69,"part":null,"text":"The trail winds deeper into the crater. The ferns thin out, and the crystals grow larger, jutting from the earth like enormous glowing teeth.\n\nProfessor Cypress pulls out a second book, bound in violet.\n\n“Same explorer. Same expedition. A very different story.”\n\nHe runs a finger down the page.\n\n“This one doesn’t blame curses. It says a meteor struck Paldea long before any king was born. The ground shifted for ages afterward, cracking and caving in, until all that remained was this crater. The jewel-like creature was caught in the upheaval and vanished.”\n\nA low mechanical hum rises from below. Far beneath you, on the crater’s floor, a laboratory blinks with light.\n\n“And at the very bottom, someone built a machine that pulls Pokémon through time.”\n\nSomething whirs out of the mist: a gleaming, metallic figure, perfectly smooth, moving with cold precision. It’s a Pokémon you almost recognize, rebuilt in steel and light. Another follows, its wings glowing white-hot.\n\nCypress takes a careful step back.\n\n“The red book saw creatures from the past. This one saw creatures from the future.”\n\nHe tucks both books away.\n\n“Curses or meteors, trainers. One of these books is wrong. Perhaps both. Whatever the truth, it’s waiting further down.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":70,"part":null,"text":"The mist swirls, and the crater vanishes. You stand in the courtyard of a grand castle, its walls lined with gold and jewels. Ancient Paldea.\n\nThe ground shudders. Four shapes emerge from the treasury, each wreathed in its own shadowy aura.\n\nProfessor Cypress grips the edge of his coat.\n\n“The relics. The red book was right.”\n\nA hulking snail cloaked in withered leaves creeps forward, trailing grudges. A sleek beast bearing a sword of ice prowls beside it, seething with hatred. A great creature covered in earth lumbers behind, and fear spreads wherever it steps. Last comes a tiny fish of fire, burning with envy.\n\n“The king’s greed made them like this. He wanted everything, and in the end, it nearly cost him everything.”\n\nShouts echo from the gates. The king’s champions charge in, warriors in gleaming armor with Pokémon at their sides. One is clad in armor steeped in old resentment. Gold coins spill from the shattered treasury and rise into the air, swirling into a single gleaming figure.\n\nCypress steps back toward you, eyes darting.\n\n“Those warriors are the heroes who sealed the relics away. But in this chaos, I don’t think they’ll stop to ask whose side we’re on.”\n\nHe lowers his voice.\n\n“Greed brought this kingdom to its knees. Don’t let it take you down with it.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":70,"part":"b","text":"The dust settles over the ruined courtyard. Wooden stakes lie scattered across the ground.\n\nAmong them, something glints.\n\nProfessor Cypress kneels and lifts a smooth stone tablet, black as a moonless night. For a moment he holds it at arm’s length, as if half-expecting it to wake up.\n\nIt doesn’t.\n\nHe lets out a breath, studies it a moment longer, and tucks it carefully into his coat.\n\n“Ten.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":71,"part":null,"text":"The castle crumbles into mist, and you’re back in the crater. This time the path leads past the humming laboratory, down a shaft that sinks deeper and deeper into the earth.\n\nWhen it finally stops, you’re nearly a kilometer underground.\n\nThe cavern before you glows. Crystals blanket every wall in every color imaginable. Massive stone Pokémon slumber among them, and towering clusters bloom like poisonous flowers.\n\nProfessor Cypress turns in a slow circle, utterly speechless.\n\n“Both books were right… and both were wrong.”\n\nIn the heart of the cavern rests what looks like a jewel. It’s small and round, no bigger than a Poké Ball.\n\n“Whatever shook this land apart, curse or meteor, one creature survived it. It wrapped itself in crystal and fell asleep. It’s been sleeping ever since.”\n\nThe jewel stirs. Tiny legs unfold from beneath it, and a small head peeks out, blinking in the light.\n\n“Two thousand years, trainers. The Paldean Empire sent expedition after expedition to find this. They called it the Hidden Treasure of Area Zero.”\n\nThe little creature yawns. Then the crystals around you begin to pulse, all at once, and its shell swells into a brilliant dome of light. It glimmers with every color, every type, every power at once.\n\nCypress shields his eyes, grinning like a child.\n\n“Look at it. Like a whole world, floating in the dark.”\n\nHe plants his feet.\n\n“No wonder the kings wanted it. Turns out this treasure hunt had a grand prize after all!”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":72,"part":null,"text":"The crystal glow fades, and cool mountain air rushes in. You stand at the foot of a great stone hall, its banners snapping in the wind. At the top of the steps a lone figure waits, his cape billowing behind him.\n\nProfessor Cypress straightens his spare glasses, suddenly very aware of his posture.\n\n“You’ll remember that strange hum beyond the trees at the stormy lake. It was a signal. Team Rocket was broadcasting it to drive the lake’s Pokémon into a frenzy.”\n\nHe nods up the steps.\n\n“One trainer followed that signal straight to their hideout and shut the whole operation down. Lance. He was raised in a clan of Dragon Masters, led the Elite Four, and rose all the way to Champion.”\n\nWings beat overhead. A sleek, ghostly dragon circles the hall, its little ones tucked against its horns, and something massive and armored in steel lands with a ground-shaking thud.\n\n“He likes to say that dragons are mythical Pokémon: hard to catch, harder to raise, but their powers are superior.”\n\nCypress looks up at the cape, then back at you, and smirks.\n\n“He also says they’re virtually indestructible. Let’s put that to the test, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":73,"part":null,"text":"The mountain hall fades, and you’re standing on a hillside above a familiar city with its great spire. But something is terribly wrong. Enormous roots coil through the streets below, cracked and withering, strangling whole buildings.\n\nProfessor Cypress shakes his head slowly.\n\n“Kalos again. Lysandre’s weapon had barely been stopped when his followers tried one last time. These roots nearly swallowed the city whole.”\n\nA blur leaps from rooftop to rooftop. Water whirls on its back, swelling into a great spinning star. With one throw, a root splits clean in two.\n\n“A Greninja. Its trainer was a boy from Pallet Town, and the bond between them grew so strong that they began to see through each other’s eyes. They felt each other’s pain and even shared a heartbeat. When their hearts beat as one, Greninja changed into something new.”\n\nHis voice softens.\n\n“When the boy went home, Greninja chose to stay behind and clear away every last root.”\n\nOverhead, a blazing falcon circles, and a masked little wrestler drops onto a rooftop, ready to help.\n\nCypress watches the Greninja land, poised and calm.\n\n“A bond like that doesn’t break just because two friends are apart. Let’s see if yours can match it, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":74,"part":null,"text":"The rebuilt city fades, and cold sea wind hits your face. You’re standing on a rocky island. An abandoned laboratory squats on the stone, its windows dark and its doors hanging open.\n\nProfessor Cypress stops at the threshold and looks back at Mew.\n\n“Have you noticed? Ever since we began, Mew has been leading us backward through time, one era after another. Not anymore. The years are jumping all over the place.”\n\nMew drifts past him into the lab without its usual somersault.\n\n“It’s following something else now, a thread. And I have a feeling every stop along it will have human fingerprints all over it.”\n\nInside, rows of glass tanks line the walls. Most are shattered. One still holds the cracked shell of a long-extinct bug Pokémon.\n\n“Three hundred million years ago, this was the most feared hunter in the world. Then it vanished. Team Plasma dug up its fossil, brought it back to life… and bolted a cannon to its back.”\n\nHis jaw tightens.\n\n“Their own king tried to shut the project down. He believed Pokémon would lose their beauty if science twisted them. Some of his scientists didn’t listen.”\n\nA red light flickers on in the dark. Something sleek and purple steps out of the shadows, its cannon humming to life.\n\nAt the far end of the room, Mew hovers beside an empty tank, very still.\n\nCypress doesn’t take his eyes off the cannon.\n\n“Bringing a Pokémon back from extinction is a miracle, trainers. Turning it into a weapon is a choice.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":74,"part":"b","text":"The humming dies. Sparks drift lazily from the ruined tanks.\n\nProfessor Cypress picks through a tray of fossil fragments and stops. Wedged among the bones is a smooth stone tablet, the bright green of a fresh leaf.\n\nFor a second he looks ready to file it with the fossils. Then he catches himself, chuckles softly, and slips it into his coat instead.\n\n“Eleven.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":75,"part":null,"text":"The sea wind softens into warm, golden light. You’re standing in the courtyard of a grand castle, banners fluttering from its towers. Somewhere inside, a crowd is cheering.\n\nProfessor Cypress smiles.\n\n“Five hundred years ago. A kingdom in celebration.”\n\nMew spins a lazy loop overhead, back to its old self.\n\nThrough an open archway, in a workshop cluttered with gears and springs, a scientist makes one last adjustment to a small, doll-like Pokémon of gleaming red and gold.\n\n“The man who built this didn’t want a weapon. He wanted a gift, a companion for the king’s daughter.”\n\nCypress taps his chest.\n\n“Its body is only a vessel. Its true self lives in here: a heart built from gathered life energy. They called it the Soul-Heart. It can’t speak a word, but it understands everything, even how you feel.”\n\nThe little Pokémon lifts its arm and sprinkles a fine mist over a pot of flowers by the window. Mew drifts closer, utterly enchanted.\n\n“Same human hands as before, trainers. A very different choice.”\n\nThen a deep rumble shakes the courtyard. Steam hisses through the castle gates.\n\n“Not everyone trusts what people make. Out on a nearby plateau, a great Pokémon of fire and water once found this little one among the flowers, and it has guarded it ever since.”\n\nHe wipes the fog from his glasses.\n\n“Mind the steam, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":76,"part":null,"text":"The castle’s golden light dims to a cold blue glow. You’re back at the bottom of the great crater in Paldea, standing at the doors of the humming laboratory you glimpsed from above. This time the doors are open.\n\nInside, an enormous machine fills the room, its core pulsing like a heartbeat.\n\nProfessor Cypress walks slowly along it, running his fingers over the controls.\n\n“So this is the machine. A brilliant professor built it, someone who dreamed of a paradise where Pokémon from the past and the future could live side by side.”\n\nWarning lights flicker across the screens.\n\n“It was a beautiful dream. But they kept the machine running long after it became dangerous. More and more creatures poured through, crowding out the Pokémon that already called this crater home. They never stopped to ask whether they should.”\n\nHe sets his hand on the cold steel.\n\n“In the end, that dream cost them everything.”\n\nMew hovers near the ceiling, silent, its tail barely moving.\n\nThe core shrieks. A wave of mist rolls out of the machine, and six shapes stride out of it.\n\nThree look like the beasts that rose from the burning tower: one like a crashing tide, one crackling with storm, one blazing like a forge. All three are ancient and wild. The other three gleam in steel and light, each one bearing blades that hum with energy.\n\nCypress backs toward you, never taking his eyes off them.\n\n“The last six it ever brought through. Stay sharp, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":77,"part":null,"text":"The machine’s shriek fades into the gentle lapping of water. You’re standing on a stone bridge in a city of canals, with lanterns glowing along the waterways and boats bobbing beneath you.\n\nBut the night sky is wrong. A streak of fire burns across it, growing brighter by the minute.\n\nProfessor Cypress pushes his spare glasses up his nose and squints.\n\n“A meteor. And it’s headed straight for us.”\n\nMew zips in frantic little circles around his head.\n\nAll along the canals, people step out of their homes and stare upward. Then, one by one, they begin to sing, softly and hopefully.\n\n“There’s an old legend about a tiny Pokémon that sleeps for a thousand years, then wakes for just seven days. If you sing to it in a pure voice, it stirs. Write your wish on one of the tags on its head, and it will come true.”\n\nHigh above the rooftops, a small golden star with fluttering paper tags blinks awake and yawns.\n\nTwo shapes streak past, one red and one blue, then shimmer and vanish into thin air.\n\n“This city has its own guardians. A pair of dragons who can bend light around their bodies until they disappear. They don’t know us yet, and they won’t let anyone near that little star until they’re sure.”\n\nCypress pulls a pencil and a scrap of paper from his coat.\n\n“Earn their trust, trainers. I’ll start writing.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":77,"part":"b","text":"The red and blue dragons soar up to meet the falling star, side by side. The little Pokémon’s tags blaze with light, and a wave of gold rolls across the sky.\n\nThe meteor shatters into a shower of harmless sparks.\n\nAlmost all of it.\n\nOne glowing fragment slips free, streaking overhead. Cypress watches it go, frowning.\n\nThen something small drops into his hands.\n\nHis glasses. His real glasses.\n\nHe stares at them. Mew somersaults with delight. High above, the tiny star giggles.\n\nCypress clears his throat, swapping one pair for the other.\n\n“I… may have added a small line at the bottom.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":78,"part":null,"text":"The canal city dissolves into the quiet hush of a harbor town at night. Boats creak gently at their moorings. There’s no moon in the sky.\n\nProfessor Cypress settles his real glasses on his nose with obvious satisfaction. Then he notices a light still burning in a small house by the docks.\n\n“A sailor lives there. His son hasn’t woken up in days. Every night, the boy is trapped in the same terrible dream.”\n\nHe points out across the black water, toward a small dark island.\n\n“A Pokémon lives out there, one that brings nightmares to anyone who wanders too close. It isn’t cruel. It’s only protecting itself. But the boy’s father sailed too near that island, and his son is the one paying for it.”\n\nA soft silver glow drifts over the waves from the opposite direction, graceful as a crescent moon.\n\n“Luckily, every nightmare has its answer. A single feather from that one can wake the boy.”\n\nMew yawns, curls up on Cypress’s shoulder, and drifts off. A moment later its tail twitches. Then its whole body flinches.\n\nMew’s eyes snap open. It stares hard at the dark horizon, toward something none of you can see.\n\nCypress strokes its head gently, a little puzzled.\n\n“Bad dream, old friend?”\n\nHe turns back to you.\n\n“Let’s go get that feather, trainers. Try not to fall asleep.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":79,"part":null,"text":"The harbor fades. You’re standing on a lonely hillside at dusk, where a tiny stone tower no taller than a person sits in the long grass. A cracked, oddly shaped stone rests at its base.\n\nProfessor Cypress kneels beside it, careful not to touch.\n\n“Five hundred years ago, a Pokémon made mischief across this land. Endless trouble, endless misdeeds. Finally, a traveler had had enough and bound it to this stone with a mysterious spell.”\n\nHe traces the crack in the air, just above its surface.\n\n“It isn’t one spirit inside. It’s a hundred and eight of them, all bound together. And some of them, the stories say, are not very nice.”\n\nA cold wind rolls over the hill. The grass bends, then goes still. Shadows begin to gather around the tower, more than the dusk can explain.\n\n“When people pass on, some spirits don’t. They linger in old masks and coffins, in hollow stumps, in lamplight and empty rooms. And something about this stone draws them in.”\n\nMew hovers close behind Cypress, peering over his shoulder.\n\nThe crack in the keystone begins to glow a sickly purple, and voices start to whisper from inside it. Dozens of them.\n\nCypress rises and backs away slowly.\n\n“Five hundred years is a long time to sit in a stone, trainers. I’d wager it’s a little cranky.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":79,"part":"b","text":"The whispering fades, and the shadows melt back into the grass.\n\nSomething glints in the crack of the keystone.\n\nProfessor Cypress works it free with two careful fingers: a smooth stone tablet, a deep twilight purple. He doesn’t put it away. He sits down in the grass and turns it over in his hands for a long time.\n\n“Twelve.”\n\nHe lets out a slow breath.\n\n“I’ve combed through every archive I can reach. I’ve compared their weight, their texture, their color against every stone on record. Nothing. Not a single mention, anywhere.”\n\nHe holds it up to the fading light.\n\n“But I’ve noticed something. Every one has been waiting exactly where we finished a battle. Never before. Never somewhere else. As if they know where we’re going.”\n\nHe glances at Mew. Mew only blinks back at him, as unreadable as ever.\n\n“And you still won’t tell me a thing, will you?”\n\nHe wraps the tablet carefully and tucks it away.\n\n“Twelve down. I’m starting to wonder how many are left.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":80,"part":null,"text":"The hillside dissolves into warm sand and salt air. You’re standing at the foot of a great stone altar on a mountaintop in Alola, the sea spread out below.\n\nOverhead, the sky is split down the middle. Half of it blazes with noon sun, and the other half glows under a full moon.\n\nProfessor Cypress shields his eyes.\n\n“Long ago, the people of Alola built altars like this one and played sacred flutes to call down two beings from another world. One was a beast that devours the sun, and the other a beast that calls the moon. They honored them as messengers of the heavens.”\n\nTwo great shapes descend. One is a radiant lion, the other a vast bat with wings like a crescent moon. Behind them, the air begins to tear, and an Ultra Wormhole yawns open.\n\n“But wherever those two travel, doors open between worlds. And Alola has guardians who don’t take kindly to open doors.”\n\nLightning cracks across the sky. Flowers burst from the stones. The waves rise, and a pink mist rolls in off the sea. Four masked figures take their places around the altar, one for each island.\n\n“The guardian deities. Fiercely protective, terribly fickle, and quick to anger.”\n\nMew drifts back toward Cypress, eyes wide.\n\nCypress tucks his hands into his coat and smiles nervously.\n\n“Sun, moon, and four angry gods, trainers. Pick your side carefully.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":80,"part":"b","text":"The wormhole seals with a soft pop. Sun and moon slide back to their proper halves of the sky.\n\nOn the altar steps, a pink mist still clings to the stone. When it lifts, a smooth tablet lies waiting, the soft pink of a seashell.\n\nProfessor Cypress picks it up and turns to show Mew.\n\nMew is busy chasing the last wisps of mist.\n\nHe laughs and tucks it away.\n\n“Thirteen.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":81,"part":null,"text":"The altar fades, and a cold wind sweeps across a rocky mountain trail. Storm clouds hang low. Somewhere ahead, two trainers are shouting at each other.\n\nProfessor Cypress hurries forward, then pulls you down behind a boulder.\n\nOne of the trainers clutches a single feather that shimmers with every color of the rainbow.\n\n“A feather from the bird of rainbow flame. You’ll remember it from the burning tower. They say only someone with a warm heart can carry one.”\n\nThe trainer shoves his own Pokémon aside without a glance. In his fist, the feather’s colors drain away until it’s black as soot.\n\n“And in the wrong hands…”\n\nShadows stretch across the trail, longer than they should be. One peels away from the ground and stands up: a small, crouched figure made of darkness, with wisps of flame flickering around it.\n\n“There’s a Pokémon that lives in the shadows of others. It slips inside them, feels what they feel, copies what they do. It has been watching over that bird’s feathers for a very long time.”\n\nIts every move matches the angry trainer’s, step for step.\n\n“Right now, it’s feeling all of that anger.”\n\nWild Pokémon creep out from the rocks, eyes dark and glinting. Among them is a pale creature with a scythe-shaped horn, the kind people once blamed for the very disasters it came to warn them about.\n\nMew flattens itself against Cypress’s shoulder.\n\n“It isn’t evil, trainers. It’s a mirror. Let’s give it something better to reflect.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":82,"part":null,"text":"The mountain trail melts into gleaming white. You’re standing on a vast artificial island floating on a calm blue sea. Everything here is spotless, quiet and a little too bright.\n\nProfessor Cypress frowns at the pristine walkways.\n\n“An organization built this place to care for Pokémon, and for the most part, it did. But once creatures started arriving through the wormholes, some of its scientists grew frightened.”\n\nHe leads you down a sterile corridor to a reinforced door. Behind thick glass, a patchwork Pokémon paces its cell, its head locked inside a heavy iron mask.\n\n“So they built a weapon. A Pokémon made from the cells of every known type, designed to hunt the beasts from other worlds. It went berserk during testing. They sealed its power away behind that mask and froze it.”\n\nMew presses its forehead against the glass and doesn’t move.\n\nCypress’s voice softens.\n\n“Thankfully, a young man took pity on it and set it free. In his care, it grew strong enough to shatter that mask on its own.”\n\nThen the lights flicker. Above the island, an Ultra Wormhole tears open. Through it you glimpse a city of towering skyscrapers, every one of them dark.\n\n“Long ago, the people of that city tried to take the light from a being made of pure radiance. They wounded it terribly. It has been in pain ever since, stealing light from anything it can reach, even from the sun and the moon.”\n\nA jagged black shape bursts through the wormhole. Its armor is clamped over a radiant lion, then over a vast moonlit bat. The two from the altar have both been taken.\n\nThen everything erupts in blinding light.\n\nCypress shields his eyes.\n\n“People built the weapon. People caused the wound. Let’s see if people can help close it, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":83,"part":null,"text":"The white island fades into silence. You’re standing in a dim stone chamber deep beneath the sand. The walls are covered in rows of small raised dots, arranged in careful patterns.\n\nProfessor Cypress runs his fingertips across them.\n\n“It’s writing, of a kind. A message left four thousand years ago by the people of Hoenn.”\n\nHe follows the dots to a massive sealed door.\n\n“They worshipped giants here, colossal Pokémon of rock, ice and steel. They honored them and prayed to them. And then they grew afraid of them.”\n\nMew drifts low along the wall, unusually quiet.\n\n“So they sealed them away. One in the desert, one in a frozen cave, one in an ancient tomb, each locked behind puzzles only the most patient could solve.”\n\nHe shakes his head.\n\n“Far away in Galar, two more were sealed together in a single ruin. One is pure electricity, wrapped in rings to hold its power back. The other was being shaped from the energy of an ancient dragon, but was never fully finished.”\n\nThe ground groans. In the dark, seven points of light flicker to life, then seven more, then seven more.\n\n“Their faces are patterns of dots, just like the writing on these walls.”\n\nThe great door begins to grind open.\n\nCypress steps back, his voice gentle.\n\n“Worshipped, then feared, then forgotten. Let’s show them someone still remembers, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":84,"part":null,"text":"The chamber’s glowing dots fade, and warmth floods in. You’re standing in a bustling village of mud-brick homes, smoke curling from a dozen chimneys. Somewhere nearby, a hammer rings against an anvil.\n\nProfessor Cypress breathes in deeply and grins.\n\n“Three thousand two hundred years ago. Can you smell that? That’s progress.”\n\nMew tumbles happily through the drifting smoke.\n\nIn the village square, a crowd has gathered around a strange Pokémon. Its body is molten silver, and its heavy arms are shaped like enormous hex nuts. Liquid metal drips from its fingertips and cools into gleaming iron at the villagers’ feet.\n\n“They revered this Pokémon because it could make iron from nothing at all. With its gift, people forged plows and pots, hammers and nails. Whole cities would one day rise on what this one Pokémon gave them.”\n\nBeside a roaring forge, a long-snouted Pokémon breathes a steady jet of flame into the coals. A blacksmith lifts a freshly hammered blade and turns it in the sunlight, admiring its edge.\n\nCypress’s smile fades a little.\n\n“Of course… iron makes more than plows.”\n\nHe watches the blade for a long moment, then shakes it off and turns to you.\n\n“Let’s enjoy the good part while it lasts, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":84,"part":"b","text":"The forge crackles quietly. A heap of scrap metal lies beside the anvil, waiting to be melted down.\n\nOn top of the pile, something glints: a smooth stone tablet, cool and steel-gray.\n\nThe blacksmith reaches for it. Professor Cypress gets there first, plucking it from the heap with an apologetic smile.\n\n“Sorry. That one’s spoken for.”\n\nHe tucks it into his coat.\n\n“Fourteen.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":85,"part":null,"text":"The village smoke thickens and turns black. You’re standing on a ridge above a wide valley, and below you two armies collide.\n\nProfessor Cypress grips the rock beside him.\n\n“Kalos, three thousand years ago.”\n\nOn one side, towering stone giants with seals across their chests march beside great iron serpents. On the other, frost-covered behemoths and armored, steel-plated brutes push back. The ground shakes with every clash.\n\n“Iron became swords. Swords strengthened armies. And armies, it turns out, also pulled Pokémon into their ranks.”\n\nHis voice drops.\n\n“Soldiers went from home to home, taking any Pokémon with power and sending it to the front. Nobody asked the Pokémon. They were tools now.”\n\nAt the edge of the valley, a forest catches fire. Small Pokémon scatter from the smoke.\n\nMew observes quietly over Cypress's shoulder.\n\n“The king of this land is a man named AZ. He has already given this war something he can never get back. Remember his name, trainers. This story is his.”\n\nThen four figures burst from the burning tree line: a steel-bodied stag, a hulking stone ox, a graceful green deer and a young colt with a single gleaming horn. They plant themselves between the fleeing Pokémon and the armies.\n\n“Across the sea, long ago, another war like this one burned another forest. Three Pokémon rose from the ashes, adopted an orphan, and swore to return whenever people’s wars threatened Pokémon again.”\n\nHe swallows.\n\n“They don’t serve either side. To them, we’re just more people. Stay close, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":86,"part":null,"text":"The burning tree line rolls away behind a wall of smoke, and you’re standing in the middle of the battlefield. Soldiers and Pokémon fight side by side.\n\nProfessor Cypress steps carefully around a broken shield.\n\n“Look at them, trainers. Most of these Pokémon weren’t dragged here. They followed the people they love, and they’re fighting for them with everything they have.”\n\nThe stone giants march past, the seals on their chests blazing.\n\n“Ancient people built those to protect them, and they’re doing exactly that.”\n\nNearby, an old dragon with a long white mane plants itself in front of a young soldier and roars at the enemy line.\n\n“That one used to come down from the mountains to play with children. Now it’s guarding one.”\n\nAll around you, Pokémon clash with fierce, relentless loyalty, each one defending someone standing behind it.\n\nMew floats calmly above the chaos. Blasts and arrows streak past it, and not one comes close. It watches every Pokémon on the field, unblinking.\n\nCypress shakes his head slowly.\n\n“Loyalty on both sides, trainers. That’s what makes a war like this so hard to stop.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":87,"part":null,"text":"The battlefield fades into the silence of a stone hall at night. A single candle burns beside an empty flowerpot.\n\nA young king sits alone at a long table covered in drawings and diagrams.\n\nProfessor Cypress hangs back in the doorway.\n\n“AZ. His Floette was a gift from his late mother. When the war came, she was sent to fight. She fought for years. Then one day she was carried home, and she never woke up.”\n\nThe king doesn’t look up. He just keeps drawing.\n\n“Grief drives people, trainers. Even good people. Sometimes it drives them to do terrible things.”\n\nBeneath the castle, a machine is taking shape: rings of iron, glass and stone, humming faintly. Ghostly lamps flicker along the walls, their pale flames drinking in the light around them.\n\n“He’s building something that can revive his fallen companion. But to power it, he needs life energy, more than should be available at any given time.”\n\nHe glances toward the window, where the distant glow of battle lights the horizon.\n\n“However, a war is the one place where life pours out every single day.”\n\nMew drifts down the stairs toward the machine, studying it closely, its tail swaying slowly.\n\nCypress watches the king’s hands move across the page.\n\n“He only wants his friend back. That’s what makes this so hard to watch.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":88,"part":null,"text":"The hall fills with light.\n\nAt the heart of the machine, a tiny Floette drifts upward, holding a flower that glows softly and will never wilt. AZ falls to his knees, laughing and weeping at once. She settles onto his shoulder as if she’d never left.\n\nProfessor Cypress smiles, then stops.\n\nThrough the castle window, along the road outside, stand rows of tall stones that weren’t there before.\n\n“Every one of those marks a Pokémon whose life went into that machine. She doesn’t know yet.”\n\nAZ carries Floette to the window. Beyond the stones, the war still burns across the horizon.\n\n“He has her back. But the war that took her is still out there. As long as it rages, it could take her again, or someone else’s Floette, or someone else’s friend.”\n\nAZ turns back to the machine and sets his hand on the controls.\n\n“He’s done grieving. Now he’s angry. If this machine has enough power to give life, it will have enough to take life. All of it, all at once, to end the war.”\n\nMew watches Floette, then the machine, silently absorbing the weight of the moment.\n\nThe machine’s glow can be seen for miles. Both armies have noticed, and they’re marching on the castle, sending their mightiest Pokémon to seize it.\n\nCypress plants himself facing the oncoming armies.\n\n“We can't change what happened that day, trainers. Hold the gates so we can at least learn from it.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":89,"part":null,"text":"The machine roars to life, and the whole castle trembles.\n\nTwo colossal beings appear above the towers. One is a radiant stag whose antlers glow with every color of life. The other is a vast crimson bird whose shadow withers the grass beneath it. Their power pours into the machine in great shimmering streams.\n\nProfessor Cypress stares up at them.\n\n“Life… and destruction. He’s harnessing both.”\n\nOn AZ’s shoulder, Floette clutches her glowing flower. Its petals shine brighter and brighter as the machine charges.\n\n“She still doesn’t know what brought her back, or what it’s about to do.”\n\nMew rises beside Cypress, perfectly calm, eyes locked on the weapon.\n\nCypress sets his feet.\n\n“Everything people have meddled with since the first sword was forged comes down to this moment. Trainers… this is the last battle of this war.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":89,"part":"b","text":"The weapon fires.\n\nA wave of light rolls across the valley. Mew raises a paw, and a shimmering barrier folds around you as the blast sweeps past.\n\nWhen the light fades, the battlefield is silent. Both armies are gone. The war is over. Floette drifts down from AZ’s shoulder and out over the field.\n\nShe passes the rows of stones along the road, slowing at each one. At the last stone she stops. Her flower, so bright a moment ago, dims.\n\nShe looks out over the empty battlefield, then back to AZ. \n\nHe holds out his hand to her, smiling, just as he did the moment she woke.\n\nFloette looks at his outstretched hand for a long moment, the same hand that built the machine. Heartbroken, she turns away, and she doesn’t come back.\n\nAZ stays exactly where he is, his hand still reaching for her, long after she’s gone.\n\nProfessor Cypress turns away from the broken man.\n\n“The weapon’s energy left him with endless life, and he would spend all of it wandering alone. His brother buried the weapon deep beneath Kalos, where it waited three thousand years for Lysandre to find it.”\n\nHe crouches and picks up a small, smooth stone. Something swirls inside it.\n\n“Wherever that light touched certain stones, they changed. They became the stones that make Mega Evolution possible. Even this ended up becoming something wonderful.”\n\nFar across the sea, the glow lights the sky over Galar. Somewhere deep below, inside a star that fell long ago, something opens its eyes.","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":90,"part":null,"text":"The valley fades, and darkness falls. Not night, but something more sinister. You’re standing on a hill in Galar beneath a sky churning black and red.\n\nProfessor Cypress looks up, his face pale in the strange light.\n\n“The same day. The light from AZ’s weapon reached all the way here.”\n\nThe ground beneath your feet begins to glow crimson.\n\n“You’ll remember the meteor that fell when the ancient Pokémon vanished. I told you something was sleeping inside it. It’s awake now.”\n\nA colossal shape rises from the earth: a skeletal dragon wrapped in crimson armor, its chest pulsing with a blinding core.\n\n“It’s starving. To stay alive, it’s draining the energy from the land itself.”\n\nAcross the hills, wild Pokémon begin to swell, growing taller than trees and then taller than towers. Their roars shake the sky.\n\n“Every Pokémon touched by its energy is growing to enormous size and losing all control. Galar’s history books call this the Darkest Day.”\n\nFrom the forest below, two great wolves burst into the open. One carries a gleaming sword in its jaws. The other is armored in a mighty shield. Two young people run at their side.\n\n“The kings who came later told this story their way: two young heroes saved Galar with a sword and a shield. Human heroes, of course. But the truth was woven into old tapestries, and the heroes were these two.”\n\nMew watches the dragon calmly, unmoved by the storm.\n\nCypress steps up beside the wolves.\n\n“One man’s weapon, and a whole other region pays the price. Stand with them, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":91,"part":null,"text":"The black storm lifts. You’re back in the valley where AZ’s war was fought, years later. The ground is gray and cracked, and nothing grows. Along the road, the tall stones still stand.\n\nProfessor Cypress kneels and lets a handful of dry dust run through his fingers.\n\n“Years after the war, and still not a single blade of grass. The land remembers.”\n\nThen a warm breeze drifts in from the sea, the first in a very long time. The clouds part overhead, and a graceful figure glides down through the gap. Wherever its shadow passes, the hard ground softens.\n\nCypress looks up, surprised.\n\n“People broke this land. But people aren’t the only ones who live here.”\n\nAt the edge of the road, a small hedgehog-like Pokémon with flowers on its back pokes its nose into the dust. With every step it takes, green spreads outward and blossoms burst open in its tracks, until the gray gives way to color.\n\nMew dives into the fresh blooms, tumbling through them and scattering petals everywhere. A tiny green sprite joins in, darting from flower to flower.\n\nCypress rises slowly, smiling.\n\n“Spring winds. Fields of flowers. And that little one only ever visits in times of peace. They can’t undo what happened here, trainers. But they’re helping the world heal from it.”\n\nHe brushes the dust from his hands.\n\n“Time for something to grow here again.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":91,"part":"b","text":"The valley is green from one end to the other. Flowers have climbed the tall stones along the road, covering them in color.\n\nAt the foot of the last stone, something glints beneath the new grass: a smooth tablet, the fresh green of spring leaves.\n\nProfessor Cypress gently parts the flowers to lift it free. Mew rolls through the blossoms beside him.\n\nHe tucks it away, smiling.\n\n“Fifteen.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":92,"part":null,"text":"The meadow fades into the clatter of hammers and saws. You’re standing in the square of a small town in Unova, half rebuilt. Fresh timber frames rise beside crumbling walls. People work quietly, heads down.\n\nProfessor Cypress looks around, pleased, then tilts his head and listens.\n\n“The fields are coming back. The people are rebuilding. But listen.”\n\nHe holds up a hand. The only sounds are hammers.\n\n“No one is singing.”\n\nAt the edge of a rooftop, a small Pokémon with long, flowing hair sits hugging its knees, silent.\n\n“That one’s melodies can stir the heart of anyone who hears them. Unova’s artists drew on its music for generations. But when the world fills with too much sorrow, its song fades away.”\n\nDown in the square, a child hums a few uncertain notes. A deep drumbeat answers as a large ape thumps out a rhythm on a tree stump. Then comes the crackling riff of an electric guitar, then a bright, rattling shake of maracas. Someone laughs. Someone else starts clapping along.\n\nOn the rooftop, the little Pokémon lifts its head.\n\nThen it begins to sing.\n\nIts voice soars over the square, and one by one, people set down their tools. As the song swells, it leaps from the roof and begins to dance, spinning faster and faster.\n\nMew twirls through the air beside it, keeping time with its tail.\n\nCypress taps his foot, beaming.\n\n“Fields can heal and walls can be rebuilt, trainers. But a place doesn’t truly come back to life until it starts singing again. Let’s give them a show.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":93,"part":null,"text":"The music fades into the howl of the wind. You’re standing at the foot of a great spiraling tower of stone, wrapped in mist.\n\n“Unova, two and a half thousand years ago.”\n\nAt the base of the tower, two young men stand face to face, shouting. They look almost exactly alike.\n\nProfessor Cypress keeps his voice low.\n\n“Twin brothers. Together they built Unova beside a single great dragon. But one brother sought truth, and the other sought ideals. Neither would give an inch.”\n\nLightning cracks overhead. Atop the tower, the great dragon writhes as a seam of light splits it down the middle.\n\n“The dragon couldn’t serve them both. So it became two.”\n\nA blazing white dragon and a crackling black dragon tear free from each other. What’s left behind slumps against the tower: a hollow, frozen shell, waiting for the parts of itself it lost.\n\nCypress turns to Mew.\n\n“Every stop along this thread has been a story about people. This one may be the oldest of them all. Two brothers who couldn’t agree, and a whole region torn apart for it.”\n\nMew gazes past the tower, toward the horizon, as if it’s already looking ahead.\n\nSomething small pops onto Cypress’s shoulder: a tiny Pokémon with big V-shaped ears, flashing a cheerful victory sign.\n\nCypress laughs in surprise.\n\n“And it seems we have an ally! They say trainers with this little one at their side never lose. Victory’s on our side today, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":93,"part":"b","text":"The thunder fades. The fire dies. The mist settles around the silent tower.\n\nThe little V-eared Pokémon tugs on Cypress’s sleeve and points at the rubble.\n\nHalf-buried in the stone is a smooth tablet, the deep blue of a twilight sky.\n\nProfessor Cypress lifts it out and gives his small friend a grateful pat.\n\n“Sixteen.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":94,"part":null,"text":"The sky above you is a brilliant starry night. You’re standing on a rocky cliff above a deep canyon, its walls laced with waterfalls.\n\n“Hoenn, two thousand years ago.”\n\nA streak of light crosses the sky. Then another. Then dozens.\n\nProfessor Cypress pushes his glasses firmly up his nose and keeps a hand pressed over them.\n\n“Oh, no you don’t. I just got these back.”\n\nHe squints upward, counting under his breath.\n\n“The one that ended the ancient Pokémon. The one beneath the crater in Paldea. The one over the canal city… This world certainly gets a lot of visitors.”\n\nOne of the meteors doesn’t burn out. Impossibly, it slows, drifting down into the canyon and glowing an eerie green. Two smaller stones tumble out after it, one shaped like a crescent moon and the other like a blazing sun. They rise into the air on their own.\n\n“Those two are found wherever meteorites land. Some people believe they came from space.”\n\nThe meteor cracks open. Something writhes inside it, tiny at first, then rapidly reshaping itself into an orange and teal figure with a crystal glowing in its chest. Its long arms shift as you watch: sharp and pointed, then broad and armored, then sleek and narrow.\n\n“Something was living inside that meteor. Something from out there.”\n\nMew drifts closer, tilting its head. The strange visitor tilts its head right back.\n\nCypress smiles.\n\n“Visitors from the stars, trainers. Let’s give them a proper welcome.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":95,"part":null,"text":"The starry canyon fades, and with it, everything familiar. There are no trees, no grass, no cities. Beneath a sky choked with smoke, molten rock glows and churns as far as you can see.\n\nProfessor Cypress turns in a slow circle, speechless.\n\n“This is the world before anything. Before people. Before forests. Before almost every Pokémon we know.”\n\nThe ground heaves. A colossal titan rises from the magma, its body traced with blazing red lines. Wherever it walks, the earth cools, hardens and climbs into mountains.\n\n“Legends say this one raised the land itself.”\n\nAt the horizon, black clouds burst open. Rain pours down in endless sheets, and a vast whale-like titan surfaces, its body traced with glowing blue. Wherever the rain falls, the land gives way to sea.\n\n“And that one filled the oceans.”\n\nThe two titans turn toward each other. Blazing sun and raging storm collide, and land and sea tear at each other where they meet.\n\n“They shaped the world between them, and they’ve fought over it ever since. More land, more sea, never enough of either.”\n\nMew watches, perfectly calm.\n\nThunder rolls from high above the clouds. A great emerald serpent descends, glowing brighter with every coil.\n\n“When those two go too far, only one can make them stop. The ruler of the skies.”\n\nThe ground lurches beneath you.\n\nCypress braces himself against a rock.\n\n“This is how the world as we know it began, trainers. Brace yourselves.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":96,"part":null,"text":"The fire and storm fade. The world is cooler now, and quieter. Scattered islands of new land drift across a gray, endless sea.\n\nThen the cold sets in. Frost creeps across the water. Snow begins to fall and doesn’t stop.\n\nProfessor Cypress pulls his coat tight, his breath clouding in the air.\n\n“The land and sea have been made. But look at it, trainers. Scattered pieces, drifting apart.”\n\nAcross the frozen sea, a herd of shaggy, tusked Pokémon trudges through the snow beside a great floating iceberg, its back as flat as a ship’s deck.\n\n“The world is entering its first ice age. These Pokémon thrived in it, for a time.”\n\nA deep rumble rolls across the ice. Through the blizzard, a colossal figure comes into view. Massive ropes are wrapped around its shoulders, pulled taut toward the horizon.\n\nBehind it, an entire landmass groans and slides across the sea.\n\nCypress stares, his mouth slightly open.\n\n“There’s an old legend that a giant towed the continents together with ropes. I always thought it was a figure of speech.”\n\nSeven points of light glow on its face, the same as the giants sealed away beneath the sand.\n\n“You’ll remember the elemental giants. It made every one of them in its own image. This is who was doing the shaping.”\n\nThe continent strikes another with a deafening crunch. Deep below, the earth grinds and strains under the weight of it.\n\nMew watches with quiet fascination.\n\nCypress tugs his scarf over his nose.\n\n“The world was raised, then filled. Now it’s being put together. Bundle up, trainers.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":96,"part":"b","text":"The blizzard eases. Snow settles softly over the newly joined land.\n\nSomething glints beneath a sheet of clear ice: a smooth tablet, the pale blue of a frozen lake.\n\nProfessor Cypress taps the ice and winces at the cold. Mew presses one tiny paw against the surface, and the ice cracks cleanly open.\n\nCypress lifts the tablet out with a grateful chuckle.\n\n“Seventeen.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":97,"part":null,"text":"The blizzard fades into darkness and heat. You’re deep underground, far beneath the place where the continents collided. The rock walls groan and creak around you, glowing red in the cracks.\n\nProfessor Cypress wipes sweat from his brow, his glasses fogging.\n\n“Every one of those continents is pressing down into spots like this one. All that weight, all that heat… with nowhere to go.”\n\nThe rock beside him shifts. Tiny crystals bloom across its surface, then wriggle free and blink at you: small gray Pokémon with gleaming jewels on their heads.\n\n“Born from heat and pressure deep beneath the earth. Some of them have slept down here for hundreds of millions of years.”\n\nA low, bubbling roar rumbles up from a chamber of molten rock below. Something crawls out of the magma, clinging to the ceiling on cross-shaped feet. Its steel body glows and drips where its own heat has melted it.\n\n“And where the heat runs hottest, the earth gives birth to something else entirely.”\n\nCypress turns back to you.\n\n“Raised, filled, put together, and now even the stones are coming to life. Pressure makes diamonds, trainers. Let’s see how you handle it.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":98,"part":null,"text":"The heat of the earth fades, and then everything fades: the ground, the sky, even the light. You’re floating in a vast, silent nothing. No up, no down. No before, no after.\n\nProfessor Cypress’s voice is barely a whisper.\n\n“Before the land, before the sea… there was this.”\n\nFar away, something stirs in the dark. It’s too distant and too enormous to make out, but it’s older than anything you’ve ever seen.\n\nFrom it, two lights burst outward. One pulses with a steady, ticking rhythm, and as it does, moments begin to pass. The other stretches wide, and suddenly there is distance, depth, somewhere to be.\n\n“Time began to move. Space began to grow.”\n\nThree smaller lights follow, darting and curious.\n\n“And three beings to keep the world’s spirit: knowledge, emotion and willpower. If time or space ever ran wild, these three could rein them in.”\n\nThen one last spark drifts out of the dark. It’s tiny, pink and playful, and it already holds a trace of every Pokémon that would ever live.\n\nCypress goes completely still.\n\nSlowly, he turns to the Mew beside him.\n\n“You were there. From the very beginning.”\n\nMew holds his gaze for a long moment. Then it nods, once.\n\nCypress lets out a shaky breath and turns back to the swirling dark, where time and space are already beginning to clash.\n\n“Whatever made all of this is still out there, trainers. But first, let’s survive its children.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":99,"part":null,"text":"Mew is shaking.\n\nBefore the void has even faded, it drifts behind Cypress’s shoulder and presses itself against his coat. In all your journeys together, you have never seen it afraid.\n\nProfessor Cypress feels it and goes still.\n\nThe darkness gives way to white walls, humming machines and blinking monitors. You’re back in the Cinnabar Lab, thirty years ago, on the night of the explosion.\n\n“I’ve read the records of this night a hundred times. They always felt… too tidy. As if someone had cut a page out.”\n\nAt the far end of the lab stands a single glass tank. Something floats inside it.\n\n“Fuji never failed. He made exactly what he was asked to make. A Pokémon born from Mew itself.”\n\nHis voice hardens.\n\n“But Silph's project had been infiltrated by Team Rocket. Giovanni wanted power, and he saw his opportunity to seize it. As you already know, something went wrong. They created something too powerful to control.”\n\nThe glass cracks. Then it shatters.\n\nThe creature inside steps out onto the lab floor. It’s tall, pale and furious. It looks at the scientists, at the machines, at everything people built to hold it, and its eyes burn.\n\n“It woke up surrounded by people who saw it as property. All it ever learned from us was that we take, and we twist, and we cage.”\n\nThe walls begin to tear apart.\n\nMew trembles, then slowly moves out from behind Cypress and floats forward, between you and the creature people made from it.\n\n“Everything we’ve seen, trainers. Every weapon, every cage, every war. This is what it costs. And Mew has carried it alone for thirty years.”\n\nCypress steps up beside his friend.\n\n“Not alone anymore.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":99,"part":"b","text":"The lab is quiet. Smoke drifts through the shattered windows.\n\nSomething glints among the broken glass of the tank.\n\nFor the first time, it’s Mew who goes to it. It lifts the smooth tablet, a soft, warm rose-pink, and carries it back to Cypress.\n\nHe takes it gently. Then he wraps his arms around his friend.\n\n“Eighteen.”\n\nThe lab fades...","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":100,"part":null,"text":"Mew leads you upward along a staircase of pure light that climbs through the clouds and past the stars, to a vast hall of white stone at the top of the world.\n\nStrange black symbols drift through the air around you, hundreds of them. They twist and circle like ancient letters that have come to life.\n\nProfessor Cypress looks around, breathless.\n\n“Every legend we’ve walked through. Every era. Every region. They all lead here.”\n\nYou feel the air around you begin to vibrate and light emanates from the entire group. One by one, the eighteen stone tablets you collected appear, circling as if to protect you.\n\nCypress stares at them, astonished.\n\n“Every one of them basically presented themselves to us. As if they knew where we were going.”\n\nAt the far end of the hall, something vast opens its eyes.\n\n“Because they weren’t just stones, were they? They were pieces of something. Watching.”\n\nThe plates streak across the hall toward the towering figure, golden-ringed and shining. One by one, they sink into its body, and with each one it blazes brighter.\n\n“The myths call it the Original One. It shaped the universe with a thousand arms. It made time and space, and knowledge, emotion and will. It made Mew.”\n\nMew reverently floats forward.\n\n“It has watched us this whole time. Every gift and every mistake, every war and every song. And now it wants to see what we’ve learned.”\n\nThe figure rises, carrying the power of every type at once.\n\nCypress straightens his glasses one last time and smiles.\n\n“This is it, trainers. Good luck.”","link":null,"status":null},{"mission":100,"part":"b","text":"The last echoes of battle fade through the great hall.\n\nThe Original One looks down at you for a long, silent moment. Then it lowers its head, just slightly, and a warm golden light washes over the group.\n\nMew turns a slow, joyful somersault, the way it used to.\n\nThe hall begins to dissolve. The stars, the clouds and the staircase of light slip away. Everything you’ve walked through flickers past: frozen continents, a valley in bloom, a singing town square, a lone figure reaching for someone who isn’t coming back.\n\nThen you’re standing on a grassy hill under an ordinary afternoon sky. It’s the same hill where all of this began.\n\nProfessor Cypress takes off his glasses, cleans them carefully on his coat and puts them back on.\n\n“When we started, I told you that knowledge written in books is not the same as knowledge lived.”\n\nHe looks at each of you, and at the Pokémon standing beside you, partners gathered from every corner of history.\n\n“Now you’ve lived it. The wonders and the wars. The wishes and the weapons. The things we broke, and the things we helped heal. History isn’t only what happened. It’s what we choose to carry forward.”\n\nMew drifts down the line of trainers and gives each of you a gentle bump with its forehead. Then it settles back onto Cypress’s shoulder.\n\nHe reaches up and rests a hand on his friend.\n\n“Thank you, old friend. For sharing all of it.”\n\nHe turns back to you and smiles.","link":null,"status":null}];

function storyFor(mission) { return STORY_DATA.filter(s => s.mission === mission && s.status !== 'Not written yet'); }
